var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Automatic-differentiation-and-Reactant","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"Simulations can be differentiated with ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff, Enzyme and Mooncake, and compiled with Reactant. Every commit is tested against every form of each backend, on the cases in test/ad_and_reactant/test_cases.jl: simulations with an environment, with sampled actions and rewards, with timestep sizes and with gradients of the sizes themselves, without states, with a state changed in place, with a selection of what is saved, with maps of names between the model and the environment, with an environment which ends the simulation, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and on single observations, and agents simulated with an environment or on single observations, with and without a history, and with a state saved with deepcopy. Code in the package's extensions is differentiated in suites of its own, each with its own environment, beside the other tests of the extension. The one for Turing.jl, in test/extensions/turing/ad, differentiates the log density of Turing.jl models of a session, in its turing_cases.jl: with the agent or the environment starting, with imputed actions, with an environment filling in observations, with an environment which doesn't run because nothing in the data is missing, with observations imputed from an environment's distributions, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and with a model which samples within each timestep, and of the full model of several sessions, also with an environment parameter shared by the sessions. That keeps the package's own code tested without Turing.jl, since the backends work without it and with other probabilistic programming languages. Every gradient is compared with finite differences. Zygote isn't supported, because the package uses for loops.\n\nBackend Forms tested\nForwardDiff default, chunk size 1\nReverseDiff default, compiled tape\nEnzyme forward, reverse, and both with runtime activity\nMooncake forward, reverse\nReactant compiled value, and compiled Enzyme reverse gradient, on the cases without a Xoshiro","title":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Known-failures","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These are bugs in the backends, which the package doesn't work around. Their tests are marked broken in runtests.jl of test/ad_and_reactant/ad and of test/extensions/turing/ad, so that the suites pass while the bug is there, and a fix shows up as an unexpected pass. Last checked with Julia 1.13.3, ReverseDiff 1.18.7, Enzyme 0.13.218, Mooncake 0.5.67, Turing 0.49.0 and DynamicPPL 0.42.14.\n\nBackend When What happens Status\nReverseDiff, with and without a compiled tape prepare_state_for_saving using deepcopy, or deepcopy of any container which holds a tracked number zero gradients, no error fixed on ReverseDiff's master branch on 2026-11-06, not yet released after v1.18.7\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity simulate when the agent starts, actions and rewards are sampled from one seeded Xoshiro, and the number of timesteps is a constant wrong gradient, no error reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme forward with runtime activity simulate! of an agent with an environment, when the model's state starts as a constant such as (; expected_reward = 0.5) wrong gradient, no error reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity the same throws a MethodError from create_shadow_ret reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity numbers copied into a vector whose element type is a Union: in simulate when an observation is NoObservation on some timesteps and a number on the others, and the observations are saved, and when a vector of timestep sizes is returned after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0, which it is by default throws an EnzymeNoTypeError reproduced without the package, to be reported; with a single timestep size, or save_initial_values = false, the sizes are right\nEnzyme forward and reverse without runtime activity the log density of a Turing.jl model of a session with an imputed action, an environment, or a model which samples within a timestep, and of the full model throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError a limitation Turing.jl documents: use runtime activity. A plain session passes\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity, and Enzyme forward when the agent starts a session whose observations are NoObservation on some timesteps, so that a row of the data is one of two NamedTuples throws an IllegalTypeAnalysisException to be reported; an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps is fine\n\nA state which depends on the parameters from the start isn't affected, and neither is simulate! on single observations. A simulation where an observation isn't made on every timestep is right on every backend when the observations aren't saved, and on Enzyme's forward mode when they are. The other forms of Enzyme, and the other backends, are right on all of these cases. ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff and Mooncake are right on every Turing.jl case, and so are both forms of Enzyme with runtime activity on a session where every column of the data has one type.","title":"Known failures"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Pitfalls","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These aren't failures of the package's own code, but a model can run into them.\n\nEnzyme reverse and SVector states: updating an SVector in a state with setindex gives wrong gradients for some sizes, with no error. A Vector changed in place or by broadcasting is right.\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity and a softmax in one line: a softmax written as exp.(v .- maximum(v)) ./ sum(exp.(v .- maximum(v))) gives wrong gradients, with no error, when it runs in a loop on values that differ between the timesteps. The softmax of LogExpFunctions.jl is right, and so is any softmax which keeps its exponentials in a variable and divides them by their sum. It is reproduced without the package, and to be reported.\nCompiled ReverseDiff tapes: a tape records one path through the code, so a model with a branch on a value gives wrong gradients at points where the other branch is taken, with no error. The tests prepare the tape at a different point to catch this.\nTuring.jl models and Enzyme: Enzyme needs runtime activity to differentiate a Turing.jl model, as Turing's own documentation says; without it, it throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError.\nTuring.jl models and Mooncake: Mooncake can't differentiate logjoint(model, values) when the model has latent variables under an indexed prefix, such as the imputed actions of a session or a model which samples within a timestep, because building the variables runs DynamicPPL's warning about growable arrays, and a @warn holds a try. Samplers go through a LogDensityFunction, where the variables exist already, and that is fine. The same try is why turing_catch_invalid_parameters is only used when asked for, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error it catches either, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no error.\nTypes known when a session is compiled: a session costs about 10 ns per timestep to evaluate, with or without latent variables, as long as every type is known when it is compiled. Earlier versions of the package built a NamedTuple from names only known at run time, which made every timestep about a hundred times slower, and that cost was taken for one of DynamicPPL.jl's. The rows of the data get the element types of their columns for the same reason, so a column which holds two types costs nothing extra. A model's own code can lose the same factor in the same way, so check a slow model with JET's @report_opt.\nReactant: the constructors of Distributions.jl don't accept traced numbers, so a model built on them can't be compiled, and sets reactant = false in its test case. An if on a traced value throws, where ifelse works. A Xoshiro can't run in a compiled function, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run, so pass a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument. Compile times grow with the number of timesteps, because the loop is unrolled, so a few hundred timesteps is the practical limit.","title":"Pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Models-from-ReinforcementLearning.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent or a learner from ReinforcementLearning.jl can do the learning inside a cognitive model. The cognitive model keeps their object in its state, gives it what was observed on each timestep, and returns the probabilities of its actions, which a simulation samples from and fitting scores the recorded actions against.\n\nThis page shows two such cognitive models. It doesn't simulate or fit them yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, because ReinforcementLearning.jl is a large package to have in the documentation's environment. It was run by hand with ReinforcementLearningCore.jl 0.15.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Categorical\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing ReinforcementLearningBase: ReinforcementLearningBase as RLBase\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: ReinforcementLearningCore as RLCore\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: Agent, QBasedPolicy, TDLearner, TabularQApproximator\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: EpsilonGreedyExplorer, PreEpisodeStage, PostActStage\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: Trajectory, CircularArraySARTSTraces\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: DummySampler, InsertSampleRatioController\n\nBoth models list the observations observation, reward and terminated, which are the names that CommonRLEnvironment gives, so they can be used with an environment from ReinforcementLearning.jl as they are. The observation must be the number of the state the environment is in, since the learners here keep a table of values.\n\nBoth also need the environment to move first on each timestep, with EnvironmentStarts, so that there is an observation to act on. On the first timestep of an episode there is no reward yet: the reward is then NoObservation, and the models check for AbstractNoValue, which covers that.","title":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#A-whole-agent","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent from ReinforcementLearning.jl reads the state, the reward and the end of the episode from an environment object, at the stages of its own run loop. This small environment holds what the cognitive model observed on a timestep, for the agent to read.\n\nstruct ObservedByAgent <: RLBase.AbstractEnv\n    state::Int\n    reward::Float64\n    terminated::Bool\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nRLBase.state(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.state\nRLBase.reward(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.reward\nRLBase.is_terminated(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.terminated\nRLBase.action_space(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = Base.OneTo(rl_env.n_actions)\n\nThe cognitive model creates the agent in its initial state, from its parameters: here a temporal difference learner with a table of action values, which explores with a probability of epsilon.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceAgent <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, parameters)\n\n    #The learner, with the learning rate and the discount factor from the parameters\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The explorer, which picks any action with a probability of epsilon\n    rl_explorer = EpsilonGreedyExplorer(parameters.epsilon; is_break_tie=true)\n\n    #The agent's memory of the last transition, which the learner learns from\n    rl_traces = CircularArraySARTSTraces(;\n        capacity=1,\n        state=Int64 => (),\n        action=Int64 => (),\n        reward=Float64 => (),\n        terminal=Bool => (),\n    )\n    rl_trajectory = Trajectory(rl_traces, DummySampler(), InsertSampleRatioController())\n\n    return (; rl_agent=Agent(QBasedPolicy(rl_learner, rl_explorer), rl_trajectory))\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the cognitive model takes the agent through the stage of its run loop that fits what was observed, and returns the probabilities its policy gives each action. The agent is changed in place, so the same object is passed on as the new state.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    rl_agent = input.previous_state.rl_agent\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = input.observation\n\n    # Update state ##\n    if reward isa AbstractNoValue\n        #An episode starts, so there is nothing to learn from yet\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, 0.0, false, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PreEpisodeStage(), rl_env)\n    else\n        #Give the agent what followed its previous action, and let it learn from it\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, reward, terminated, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PostActStage(), rl_env, input.previous_action.move)\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_agent, PostActStage())\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The policy gives a distribution over the actions, which the action is sampled from\n    Pₜ = RLBase.prob(rl_agent.policy, rl_env)\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_agent))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :epsilon),\n        states=:rl_agent,\n        observations=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        actions=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nSince the agent is changed in place, every saved state would be the same object, showing the values after the last timestep. This method saves a copy of the table of action values on each timestep instead.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, state)\n    return (; action_values=copy(state.rl_agent.policy.learner.approximator.model))\nend","title":"A whole agent"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Only-the-learner","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"A learner can also be used on its own, with one transition at a time, which needs no environment object and no trajectory. The action probabilities are then the cognitive model's own: here a softmax over the learner's action values, with an inverse temperature. The softmax is the one from LogExpFunctions.jl.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceLearner <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, parameters)\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The learner needs the observation before the action too, so that is kept in the state\n    return (; rl_learner, previous_observation=0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    (; rl_learner, previous_observation) = input.previous_state\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = input.observation\n    β = input.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Let the learner learn from what followed the previous action, unless an episode starts\n    if !(reward isa AbstractNoValue)\n        rl_transition = (;\n            state=previous_observation,\n            next_state=observation,\n            action=input.previous_action.move,\n            reward,\n            terminal=terminated,\n        )\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_learner, rl_transition)\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The learner's value of each action where the agent is now\n    Qₜ = RLCore.forward(rl_learner, observation)\n    #Softmax over the action values\n    Pₜ = Categorical(softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_learner, previous_observation=observation))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :inverse_temperature),\n        states=(:rl_learner, :previous_observation),\n        observations=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        actions=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, state)\n    return (;\n        action_values=copy(state.rl_learner.approximator.model), state.previous_observation\n    )\nend","title":"Only the learner"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Fitting","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive models yet. When they are fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for both cognitive models here, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl can't differentiate either of them, because the learner keeps its learning rate and its discount in Float64 fields, which the number types of those two don't fit in.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax can get a wrong gradient from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/cognitive_abm_with_agents/#Cognitive-agent-based-modelling-with-Agents.jl","page":"Cognitive agent-based modelling with Agents.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to put a CognitiveAgent into an agent-based model from Agents.jl, with what each agent observes in the world and what its actions do to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Cognitive agent-based modelling with Agents.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"These are only used inside the package, and can change without notice. What users work with is on the page API.\n\nPages = [\"internals.md\"]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel","page":"Internals","text":"AbstractModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models and environment models, which every function that a model implements or is used with takes. A model is a subtype of AbstractCognitiveModel or of AbstractEnvironmentModel, and not of this type itself.\n\nThe two kinds work the same way: on each timestep a model receives an input and gives an output, in a ModelContext and a ModelOutput. In a simulation or a fitted session, the outputs of the cognitive model are its actions, which are the inputs of the environment model, and the outputs of the environment model are the observations, which are the inputs of the cognitive model. A cognitive model can therefore also serve as the environment of another.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.as_tuple_of_names-Tuple{Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"as_tuple_of_names(names)\n\nReturn names as a Tuple of Symbols, which is how a ModelAttributes keeps them: a tuple as it is, a single Symbol in a tuple, and a Vector of them as a tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_tuple_of_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Internals","text":"check_unique_attribute_names(attributes)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if a name in attributes, a ModelAttributes, is used for more than one attribute, within a kind or across the kinds, and return nothing otherwise. Whether the names are unique is decided from the type, by unique_names, so that the check costs nothing when they are, and the message is only made when they aren't. Used by the constructor of ModelAttributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Internals","text":"throw_duplicate_attribute_name(attributes)\n\nThrow the ArgumentError of check_unique_attribute_names, naming the first name of attributes that is used twice and the kinds it is used for. It is only called when there is one, so it doesn't need to be fast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.unique_names-Tuple{Tuple{}}","page":"Internals","text":"unique_names(names)\n\nReturn whether no name in the tuple names appears twice. It is unrolled over the names with has_name, so that the compiler decides it from the names alone when it knows them, which allunique doesn't guarantee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.unique_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_input_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_input_names(model, input)\n\nThrow an informative error if input, given to a model without the other side of a simulation, isn't a NamedTuple with every input that attribute_names lists for model. It can have more than those.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_input_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_match_attribute_names(model, parameters, state, previous_output)\n\nThrow an informative error if the names of the parameters, of the previous output, or of a state that is a NamedTuple, aren't those that attribute_names lists for model, a cognitive model or an environment model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit","page":"Internals","text":"check_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, observation_map=nothing,\n                action_map=nothing)\n\nThrow an informative error if cognitive_model and environment can't be simulated together. Without a map, the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other in attribute_names: the observations the environment gives are what the cognitive model lists as its inputs, and the actions the cognitive model gives are what the environment lists as its inputs. With a NameMap for the observations or the actions, the map must take names the giver lists, and give exactly the names the receiver lists.\n\nThe names are in the types, so the check is decided at compile time and costs nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_observations_fit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"check_observations_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                       environment_names, observation_map)\ncheck_actions_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                  environment_names, action_map)\n\nThrow an informative error if the observations environment gives, its outputs, don't fit those cognitive_model lists as its inputs, or the actions cognitive_model gives, its outputs, don't fit those environment lists as its inputs, as check_names_fit describes. The methods for a NameMap are with the simulation code, where that type is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_observations_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_type_attribute_names(attributes, model)\n\nReturn attributes if it is the ModelAttributes that a method of attribute_names must return, and throw an informative error otherwise. model is the cognitive model or the environment model whose method returned it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.describe_model-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Internals","text":"describe_model(model)\n\nReturn how model is named in an error message: as a cognitive model or as an environment model, with its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"has_attribute_names(model)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, has its own method of attribute_names, instead of the fallback which throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_name-Tuple{Symbol, Tuple{}}","page":"Internals","text":"has_name(name, names)\nhas_every_name(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether name is among the tuple names, and whether every name in the tuple names is among other_names.\n\nBoth are unrolled over the names, so that the compiler decides them from the names alone when it knows them, which it doesn't for issubset and issetequal with more than one name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initial_output-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"initial_output(model)\ninitial_input(model)\n\nReturn the output and the input from before the first timestep: a NamedTuple with the output or the input names that attribute_names lists for model, a cognitive model or an environment model, each BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initial_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.model_header-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Internals","text":"model_header(model)\n\nReturn the first line that show prints for a model: which kind of model it is. Used by the show method of an AbstractModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.model_header"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.outputs_before_first_timestep-Union{Tuple{ModelAttributes{Tparameters, Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs}}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}, Tuple{Tparameters}} where {Tparameters, Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs}","page":"Internals","text":"outputs_before_first_timestep(attributes)\ninputs_before_first_timestep(attributes)\n\nReturn a NamedTuple with the output or the input names in attributes, a ModelAttributes, each BeforeFirstTimestep. The names are read from the type, so that the NamedTuple is known at compile time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.outputs_before_first_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names-Tuple{IO, ModelAttributes}","page":"Internals","text":"print_attribute_names(io, attributes)\n\nPrint a line for each kind of attribute in attributes, a ModelAttributes, with none if it has no names of that kind.\n\nThe show methods of a ModelAttributes, of a cognitive model and of an environment model each print a header of their own, and then these lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.same_names-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"same_names(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether two tuples of names have the same names, in any order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.same_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.as_selection-Tuple{Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"as_selection(names)\n\nReturn names as a selection, which is how a SaveHistory keeps it: a Tuple of Symbols, made from a single Symbol or a Vector of them too, or SaveEverything as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names-Tuple{SaveHistory, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_savehistory_selection_names(save_history, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if save_history selects an input, an output or an output distribution by name that attribute_names of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, doesn't list, since a history starts from those names. The states aren't checked, because prepare_state_for_saving can save anything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_selected_names-Tuple{SaveEverything, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_selected_names(selection, names, kind, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if selection has a name that isn't among names, those that model lists for kind, which is \"inputs\", \"outputs\" or \"output distributions\", the last being named like the outputs. SaveEverything can always be selected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_selected_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.save_state-Tuple{Any, Any, Val}","page":"Internals","text":"save_state(model, state, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what is saved of state: what selection selects of the output of prepare_state_for_saving. When nothing is selected, the state isn't prepared at all, so that no copy is made of a state which isn't saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.save_state"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes-Tuple{Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_steps, save_history)\n\nReturn the sizes of n_steps timesteps as they are returned with a history: one size for each timestep when save_history saves anything, after BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when it saves the initial values, and an empty vector when it saves nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_values-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_values}","page":"Internals","text":"saves_initial_values(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history saves the values from before the first timestep, as timestep 0. It is read from the type, so that it is known at compile time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{(), (), (), (), Tsave_initial_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_values}} where Tsave_initial_values","page":"Internals","text":"saves_nothing(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history selects nothing of any kind, which is known at compile time. Whether it saves the initial values doesn't matter, since there is nothing to save of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.select_names-Tuple{Any, Val{SaveEverything()}}","page":"Internals","text":"select_names(x, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what a SaveHistory selects of x: x itself for SaveEverything, an empty NamedTuple for no names, and the named entries of a NamedTuple for a tuple of names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.state_selection-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_values}","page":"Internals","text":"state_selection(save_history)\ninput_selection(save_history)\noutput_selection(save_history)\noutput_distribution_selection(save_history)\n\nReturn the selection of one kind in a SaveHistory as a Val, so that the names are known at compile time wherever something is selected with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.state_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.given_name-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"given_name(name)\nreceived_name(name)\n\nReturn the name an output is given under and the name it is received under, from one argument of a NameMap: the two sides of a :given => :received pair, or a single Symbol, which is received under the name it is given with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.given_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.starting_history-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Internals","text":"starting_history(model, state, previous_output, save_history)\n\nReturn the history that an InstantiatedModel starts with: a ModelHistory with what save_history selects of where it starts, as timestep 0, and BeforeFirstTimestep as the size of the timestep, since none has run yet. When nothing is selected, or save_history doesn't save the initial values, the vectors are empty.\n\nA model hasn't received anything when it starts, so that entry has every input the model lists as BeforeFirstTimestep(), and so has every output distribution. reset! starts from the same history as the constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.starting_history"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_model_output-Tuple{ModelOutput, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_model_output(output, model)\n\nReturn output if it is the ModelOutput that an apply_model method of model must return, and throw an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_model_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_not_ended-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_not_ended(model, state, parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if stopping_criterion of model is already met with state, before the first timestep of a simulation, since no timestep can then run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_not_ended"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_outputs-Tuple{Val{false}, Vararg{Any, 7}}","page":"Internals","text":"collect_initial_outputs(save_initial_values::Val, previous_outputs, cognitive_model,\n                        environment, observation_map, action_map,\n                        cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nReturn what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, in a NamedTuple with the names of the saved outputs of collect_timestep_outputs, or nothing when save_initial_values is Val(false).\n\nThe observation and the action are the previous ones, as each side receives them, the output distributions are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output, since none was given yet, and the states are the states before the first timestep. They must be saved before the first timestep runs, since it can change a state in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_outputs-NTuple{10, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"collect_timestep_outputs(environment_output, cognitive_output,\n                         observation_from_environment, observation, action,\n                         action_to_environment, cognitive_model, environment,\n                         cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nCollect the outputs of a timestep in a NamedTuple, each both as it is, which the next timestep starts from, and as it is saved, which cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select.\n\nobservation_from_environment is the observation sampled from what the environment gave, and observation is the same observation as the cognitive model receives it. action_to_environment is action as the environment receives it. Each pair differs when a NameMap selects or renames them. The cognitive model's history holds the observation as the cognitive model receives it, and the environment's the action as the environment does.\n\nThis is its own function so that if an output can have two types, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoObservation, the compiler can split the call and get a concrete type for each.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"each_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nReturn the size of each of n_steps timesteps as a vector, for a history. A vector of sizes is returned as it is, and anything else is repeated for every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"get_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, t)\n\nReturn the size of timestep t. A vector has one size for each timestep, and anything else is the size of all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_ended-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"has_ended(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n          outputs)\n\nReturn whether stopping_criterion of either model is met, with the states in outputs, the outputs of the timestep before. Used by the simulation loop before each timestep, when the criteria are checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_ended"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage-Tuple{Nothing, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"initialize_all_storage(initial_outputs, outputs, n_steps)\n\nCreate the NamedTuple of vectors for saving the outputs of n_steps timesteps, typed by outputs, the outputs of the first timestep. When initial_outputs isn't nothing, each vector starts with what it has for timestep 0, from collect_initial_outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"initialize_storage(first_value, n_steps)\ninitialize_storage(initial_value, first_value, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vector for saving one kind of output over n_steps timesteps, using the value of the first timestep to set its type. With initial_value, what is saved from before the first timestep, the vector starts with it, as timestep 0, and its type holds both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.map_names-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"map_names(x, name_map)\n\nReturn what the receiver gets of the outputs x: all of x without a map, and with a NameMap the outputs it takes, under the names it gives them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"merge_default_parameters(model, parameters)\n\nReturn parameters with the values from default_parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, for the parameters it doesn't have, if model provides defaults for them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.push_output!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"push_output!(storage, value)\n\nAdd value at the end of storage, and return the vector. As for store_output!, a value that doesn't fit the type of storage moves the outputs so far to a wider vector, which is returned instead. Starting from Vector{Union{}}() lets the first value set the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.push_output!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, Val{false}}","page":"Internals","text":"returned_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_timesteps, save_initial_values::Val)\n\nReturn the sizes of the n_timesteps timesteps that ran, as a simulation returns them. A vector of sizes is cut to those timesteps, since a model can end a simulation early, and starts with BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when save_initial_values is Val(true), so that it has an element for each element of what is saved. Anything else is returned as it is, since it is the size of every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.run_remaining_timesteps-NTuple{16, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"run_remaining_timesteps(storage, outputs, who_starts, cognitive_model, environment,\n                        cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                        cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n                        observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_sizes, n_steps,\n                        offset, check_stopping_criterion)\n\nRun the timesteps after the first, and return the NamedTuple of vectors with the saved outputs of the timesteps that ran, followed by the outputs of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the timesteps end before n_steps if stopping_criterion of either model is met. offset is 1 when the vectors start with timestep 0, and 0 otherwise, so that timestep t is element t + offset.\n\nThis is its own function so that it's compiled for the types of the first outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.run_remaining_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.run_timesteps-NTuple{15, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"run_timesteps(previous_outputs, who_starts, cognitive_model, environment,\n              cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, cognitive_save_history,\n              environment_save_history, observation_map, action_map, rng,\n              timestep_sizes, n_steps, save_initial_values::Val,\n              check_stopping_criterion)\n\nRun up to n_steps timesteps starting from previous_outputs, and return a NamedTuple of vectors with the saved outputs of the timesteps that ran, followed by the outputs of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the timesteps end before n_steps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, and a model where it is met already can't run any. When save_initial_values is Val(true), each vector starts with what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0.\n\nThe vectors are states, observations, actions and action_distributions with what cognitive_save_history selects of the cognitive model's, and environment_states, environment_observations, environment_actions and environment_observation_distributions with what environment_save_history selects of the environment's, as saved_values arranges them for what is returned.\n\nsimulate! continues from the last outputs instead of from what was saved, because prepare_state_for_saving can return anything, and because the next simulation can change a state in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.run_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.sample_output-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"sample_output(rng, output_distribution)\n\nSample an output from what a model returned, using rand. Each output in a NamedTuple is sampled on its own, so a NamedTuple inside it gives a NamedTuple of samples, and an entry that is NoAction or NoObservation is kept. A number, a string or a symbol is a value and not something to sample from, and throws an ArgumentError which names Deterministic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sample_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_values-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_values(storage, timestep_sizes)\n\nReturn the saved values of a simulation as simulate returns them, from the NamedTuple of vectors in storage: the cognitive model's under states, observations, actions and action_distributions, the environment's under environment, and timestep_sizes as given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.store_all_outputs!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"store_all_outputs!(storage, outputs, t)\n\nSave the saved outputs of timestep t in outputs, from collect_timestep_outputs, as element t of each vector in storage, with store_output!, and return the NamedTuple of vectors, in which a vector is wider when a value didn't fit it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_all_outputs!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.store_output!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"store_output!(storage, value, t)\n\nSave value as element t of storage, and return the vector.\n\nIf the value doesn't fit the type of storage, the outputs so far are moved to a wider vector which can hold both types, and that vector is returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_output!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(model, input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size, rng,\n         save_history)\n\nRun a timestep of model on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the output sampled from what it gave, its new state, and what save_history selects of the state, the input, the output and the output distribution, in a NamedTuple. The input must have the names that model lists.\n\nsimulate and simulate! on a single input both run this. It is its own function so that it's compiled for the types in an instantiated model's untyped fields, which makes simulate! on a single input several times faster, and makes the check of the input's names cost nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-Tuple{AgentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(::AgentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n         environment_parameters, cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n         observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nRun a timestep where the agent moves first, starting from the outputs of the timestep before, and return its outputs.\n\nThis is always inlined, because a call here makes the loop several times slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-Tuple{EnvironmentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(::EnvironmentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n         environment_parameters, cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n         observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nRun a timestep where the environment moves first, starting from the outputs of the timestep before, and return its outputs.\n\nThis is always inlined, because a call here makes the loop several times slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_session-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"prepare_session(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nReturn the SessionInfo of one session, with the checks of the data and the names, the replacements of missing in the data, whether the environment runs, and what is scored and returned prepared, as its constructor describes. Used by SessionInfo and MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.build_column-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"build_column(table, specification, rows)\n\nReturn the column of an attribute at the rows rows of table, as columns_used describes the specification: the column itself, or one built row by row from several, where a row with a missing in any of them gives missing. The column is narrowed as a whole first, see narrow_column, so that every session gets the same element type. Used by select_rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_column"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_data-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"check_data(cognitive_model, observations, actions)\n\nThrow an informative error if the data of a session isn't a NamedTuple of vectors for the observations and one for the actions, doesn't have the observations and the actions that attribute_names lists, or has columns of different lengths, and return the number of timesteps otherwise. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, NoEnvironment, NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"check_environment_fits(cognitive_model, environment, observations)\n\nThrow an informative error if a session can't run with environment: without one, the observations in the data can't have a MissingValue, since nothing fills it in, and with one, it must list the same observations and actions as cognitive_model. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, <:CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}, NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"check_environment_parameters(session_info, environment_parameters)\ncheck_environment_parameters(sessions_info, environment_parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if environment parameters are given for a session, or for sessions, without an environment model, which is decided by the types. Used by the Turing.jl model of several sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_table-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"check_table(table, columns)\n\nThrow an informative error if table isn't a NamedTuple of vectors of one length, or doesn't have every column in columns. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_table"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.column_map-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"column_map(columns, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the columns of kind, :observations or :actions, as a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column: a NamedTuple as it is, and a tuple or a vector of names as the same names in both places. A single name is the column of the model's only attribute of that kind, whatever the column is called. It is an error when the model lists several, and when the model lists the name as an attribute of the other kind only, since observation_columns and action_columns may then be swapped. When the model lists none, it is a column of that name. A specification is the name of a column, or how to build one from several, see columns_used. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_map"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.columns_used-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"columns_used(specification)\n\nReturn the names of the columns of a table that one specification of an attribute's column uses, as a tuple. A specification is one of:\n\na Symbol, the column itself;\na NamedTuple of Symbols, such as (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol), for a column of NamedTuples with those names built from the columns, one for each row;\na Tuple of Symbols, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol), for a column of Tuples;\na Pair of a Tuple of Symbols and a function, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol) => (choice, rt) -> [choice, rt], for a column with what the function gives for the values of each row.\n\nA missing in any column used makes the value of that row missing. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it checks a table, and by build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.columns_used"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.data_names-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"data_names(cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the names that attribute_names of cognitive_model lists for the data of kind, :observations or :actions: its inputs for the observations and its outputs for the actions. Used by column_map and named_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.data_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.entry-Union{Tuple{name}, Tuple{Val{name}, Any}} where name","page":"Internals","text":"entry(::Val{name}, value)\n\nReturn (; name = value), built in a call of its own, so that a value which can have two types gives one of two NamedTuples with a concrete field each, as (; a = x) written out doesn't: Julia infers that as a NamedTuple whose field type is the Union, which nothing is compiled for, while a call with a Union-typed argument is split by type. A NamedTuple which holds such a value is built with merge of these, as row does. Used by row, by saved_session_outputs and by the Turing.jl models of a timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping-Tuple{Nothing, NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"first_timestep_to_check_stopping(check_stopping_criterion, observations, actions,\n                                 n_timesteps)\n\nReturn the first timestep before which a session checks the stopping_criterion of its models, from the check_stopping_criterion of SessionInfo: the timestep after the last one with data when it is nothing, which is 1 when every value is MissingValue, 1 when it is true, and n_timesteps + 1 when it is false, so that no timestep is checked. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}}","page":"Internals","text":"has_environment(session_info)\nhas_environment(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether a SessionInfo has an environment model, or every session of a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which is decided by the type. A session can have an environment which doesn't run, see SessionInfo. Used by check_environment_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"has_missing_entries(columns)\n\nReturn whether any entry of columns, the observations or the actions in the data of a session with one vector for each name, is MissingValue. Used by SessionInfo, to decide whether the environment runs, and by check_environment_fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"has_missing_value(data)\n\nReturn whether any entry of data, the NamedTuple of the actions or the observations of one timestep, is MissingValue. It is decided at compile time when no entry can be. Used by the Turing.jl models of a timestep, to skip imputing when nothing is missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initial_output_of_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}}","page":"Internals","text":"initial_output_of_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the output of the environment of a session from before the first timestep, every observation it lists as BeforeFirstTimestep, or an empty NamedTuple without an environment. Used by initial_session_outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initial_output_of_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initial_session_outputs-Tuple{Val{true}, SessionInfo, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"initial_session_outputs(save_initial_values::Val, session_info, previous)\n\nReturn what a session saves from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, when save_initial_values is Val(true), and nothing otherwise. The distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output of each model, since none was given yet. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, before its first timestep runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initial_session_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_distributions-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"initialize_distributions(recorded_distributions)\n\nReturn an empty vector for the distributions of each output that is recorded in a session, typed by the distribution the model gave on the first timestep, so that the vectors keep one type through the session when every timestep gives the same type. An output the model doesn't give on the first timestep gets a Vector{Union{}}(), which the first distribution kept sets the type of, as for push_output!. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, after its first timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_distributions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_session_storage-Tuple{Nothing, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"initialize_session_storage(initial_saved, saved, n_timesteps)\n\nReturn the vectors for what a session saves over n_timesteps timesteps, one for each entry of saved, what the first timestep saved, and typed by it, as initialize_storage makes them. They start with initial_saved, what timestep 0 saved, when it isn't nothing. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, after its first timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_session_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.is_recorded-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"is_recorded(value)\n\nReturn whether a single action or observation in the data happened and was recorded, so that it is scored: neither MissingValue nor a marker for nothing happening, such as NoAction or NoObservation. Used by recorded_values and recorded_on_timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_recorded"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"last_timestep_with_data(columns)\n\nReturn the last timestep on which any column in columns, a NamedTuple of vectors, has a value that isn't MissingValue, and 0 when none has. A marker for nothing happening counts as data, since it says that the timestep happened. Used by first_timestep_to_check_stopping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.named_data-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"named_data(data, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the data of kind, :observations or :actions, as a NamedTuple of vectors: a single vector as the data of the model's only attribute of that kind, which is an error when the model lists another number of them, and anything else as it is, for check_data to check. A vector of NamedTuples with the model's names is the data written as one row for each timestep, which is returned as it is, for check_data to report. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.named_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.narrow_column-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"narrow_column(column)\n\nReturn column with the types it holds as its element type, when its element type is Any or another abstract type that isn't a Union, and column itself otherwise. A vector written as [true, MissingValue(), true] has the element type Any, since only missing is promoted to a Union, and an Any in the data makes every timestep run with its types unknown. Used by replace_missing and select_rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.narrow_column"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.parameters_by_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"parameters_by_session(values_by_session, session_ids)\n\nReturn the values of each session in values_by_session, a NamedTuple with an entry for each session named by its identifier as session_symbol names it, as one vector for each parameter with a value for every session, in the order of session_ids. A session without values, or with other parameters than the first, is an error. Used by the Turing.jl model of FixedParametersBySession.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameters_by_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.recorded_action_names-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"recorded_action_names(session_info)\nrecorded_observation_names(session_info)\n\nReturn the names of the actions, or of the observations, that have recorded values in the data of a session, which are scored against the distributions the cognitive model, or the environment model, gave for them, in a Val, so that the timesteps are compiled knowing them. A session without an environment scores no observations, so it has no such names. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, to select the distributions it scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.recorded_action_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.recorded_on_timestep-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, Any}} where names","page":"Internals","text":"recorded_on_timestep(data_output, output_distribution, model)\n\nReturn whether each output of model on one timestep has a recorded value in the data, as a NamedTuple of Bools with the names of data_output: the actions of a cognitive model, or the observations an environment model gives. A recorded value is scored against the distribution the model gave for it in output_distribution, so it is an error when the model gave nothing for it, NoAction or NoObservation. Used by the Turing.jl model of a timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.recorded_on_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.recorded_values-Union{Tuple{NamedTuple{names}}, Tuple{names}} where names","page":"Internals","text":"recorded_values(columns)\n\nReturn the recorded values of each action or observation in columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, as a NamedTuple of vectors in the order of the timesteps. Fitting scores these against the distributions the model gave on the same timesteps: the actions against the cognitive model's, and the observations against the environment's. Each vector has the element type of its column without MissingValue and the markers for nothing happening, so that a session without recorded values for a name gets an empty vector of the same type as other sessions, which scores nothing. A name whose column can only hold those markers is left out, which is decided by its type, so that it is the same for every session of a table. Used by SessionInfo, which keeps the results in its fields recorded_actions and recorded_observations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.recorded_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.replace_missing-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"replace_missing(columns, replacement)\n\nReturn columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, with every missing replaced by replacement: MissingValue for a value that wasn't recorded, or NoAction or NoObservation for nothing happening on that timestep. A column whose element type allows missing gets the replacement in its element type whether it holds a missing or not, so that the sessions of one table all have the same type, and a column whose element type doesn't is kept as it is, after narrow_column. Anything that isn't a NamedTuple of vectors is returned as it is, for check_data to report. Used by SessionInfo on the observations and the actions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.replace_missing"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.row-Tuple{NamedTuple{()}, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"row(columns, t)\n\nReturn the NamedTuple of the values at timestep t of columns, a NamedTuple of vectors with one entry for each timestep, built with entry so that a column which holds two types, such as an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps, gives one of two NamedTuples with concrete fields rather than one without a definite type. It is unrolled over the names, which it takes from the type. Used by the Turing.jl models of a timestep and of several sessions, which reads the parameters of one session with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.row"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"rows_per_session(table, id_columns)\n\nReturn a vector with one pair for each session in table: its identifier, and the indices of its rows. A session is every row with one combination of the values in id_columns, in the order of the first row of each. The identifier is the value itself for a single column, and a NamedTuple of the values for several. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.running_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"running_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session with its environment running when it has one, and everything else the same. Used by sessions_for_environment_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.running_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.samples_parameters-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"samples_parameters(parameters_model)\n\nReturn whether a parameters model samples the parameters it gives, which is decided by its type: false for NoParameters, FixedSessionParameters and FixedParametersBySession, and true for every other kind, also those of one's own. Used by sessions_for_environment_parameters and warn_if_parameter_names_overlap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.samples_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_cognitive_outputs-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_cognitive_outputs(cognitive_model, outputs, save_history)\n\nReturn what save_history selects of the cognitive model's part of the outputs of one timestep: state, observation, action and action_distribution. Used by saved_session_outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_cognitive_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_session_outputs-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_session_outputs(session_info, outputs)\n\nReturn what a session saves of the outputs of one timestep, or of where it starts, as the save histories of session_info select it, in a NamedTuple with the cognitive model's state, observation, action and action_distribution, and with an environment its environment_state, environment_observation, environment_action and environment_observation_distribution. The observation and the action can have one of two types, so they go in through entry. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_session_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_values_until-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_values_until(storage, n_saved)\ntimestep_sizes_until(timestep_sizes, n_saved)\n\nReturn the vectors in storage, what a session saved, each cut to its first n_saved elements, and the vector of timestep sizes cut the same way, for a session that ended before its data did when a stopping_criterion was met. An empty vector of sizes, for a session that saves nothing, is kept. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_values_until"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.select_rows-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"select_rows(table, columns, rows)\n\nReturn the columns in columns, a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column in table, at the rows rows, as a NamedTuple with the model's names, each built with build_column. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"select_timestep_sizes(table, timestep_sizes, rows)\n\nReturn the sizes of the timesteps of one session: the column named timestep_sizes of table at the rows rows, or timestep_sizes itself when it is a size for all. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.session_id-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Symbol}, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"session_id(table, id_columns, i)\n\nReturn the identifier of the session that row i of table belongs to. Used by rows_per_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_id"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.session_saved_values-Tuple{Any, Any, SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}}","page":"Internals","text":"session_saved_values(storage, timestep_sizes, session_info)\n\nReturn what a session saved as turing_behavioural_session returns it, from the vectors in storage: the cognitive model's under states, observations, actions and action_distributions, the environment's under environment when the session has one, and timestep_sizes. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, at its end.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_saved_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.session_symbol-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"session_symbol(session_id)\n\nReturn the identifier of a session as a Symbol: the value of a single session column, and the values of several joined by underscores, as in S1_2. Used to name the entries of session_values_chain and to find the values of each session in FixedParametersBySession.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_symbol"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.sessions_for_environment_parameters-Tuple{Any, MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"sessions_for_environment_parameters(environment_parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nReturn the sessions as a turing_full_model runs them with environment_parameters_model: as they are when it samples nothing, and otherwise with every session that has an environment running it, so that the observations in the data inform the parameters. Used by turing_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sessions_for_environment_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"sessions_save_anything(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether the save history of either model of any session in sessions_info selects anything. Used by warn_if_saving_without_downstream_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.store_distributions-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}} where names","page":"Internals","text":"store_distributions(distributions, recorded_distributions, recorded_on_timestep)\n\nAdd the distributions of the outputs that are recorded on one timestep, where recorded_on_timestep says which, to the vectors in distributions, one for each output that is recorded in the session, and return them. A distribution of another type than its vector widens the vector, as for push_output!. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_distributions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.store_session_outputs!-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"store_session_outputs!(storage, saved, i)\n\nStore saved, what one timestep saved, as element i of each vector in storage, and return the storage, which store_output! widens when a value has a new type. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session, on every timestep after the first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_session_outputs!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.warn_if_parameter_names_overlap-Tuple{Any, Any, MultipleSessionsInfo, Bool}","page":"Internals","text":"warn_if_parameter_names_overlap(cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model,\n                                sessions_info, include_submodel_prefixes)\n\nWarn when the cognitive model and the environment model of the sessions list a parameter of the same name, both parameters models sample, and the variables have no prefixes, since their variables can then have the same names, and a name that both use is one variable, which Turing.jl's sample rejects. Whether both sample is decided by their types. Used by turing_full_model when it creates the model, outside the model's body, which is differentiated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.warn_if_parameter_names_overlap"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.warn_if_saving_without_downstream_model-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"warn_if_saving_without_downstream_model(sessions_info, downstream_model)\n\nWarn when a session in sessions_info saves anything and downstream_model is NoDownstreamModel, since saving costs about 5 % of each gradient and nothing in the model uses the saved values: they are read after the fit with session_values_chain, from sessions that save nothing. Used by turing_full_model when it creates the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.warn_if_saving_without_downstream_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"with_empty_environment_entry(saved_values)\n\nReturn saved_values, what a session without an environment saved, with an environment entry whose vectors hold an empty NamedTuple for each saved timestep, for a session which has an environment but didn't run it, so that every session with an environment returns the same entries, in the same order as session_saved_values gives them, since the model's return otherwise has two types. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.without_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"without_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session with NoEnvironment() as its environment and everything else the same, so that it runs as a session without an environment. Used by the Turing.jl model of a session whose environment doesn't run, since every observation is in the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.without_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.without_missing_actions-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"without_missing_actions(actions)\n\nReturn actions, a NamedTuple with one vector per action, with every MissingValue replaced by NoAction, for a session where a missing action is passed on as an action that wasn't taken instead of being imputed. As for replace_missing, a column gets NoAction in its element type when the element type allows a MissingValue, whether it holds one or not, and is kept as it is otherwise. Used by SessionInfo when impute_missing_actions is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.without_missing_actions"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Planned-tutorials-and-interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"This page lists what is planned and not written yet. The list will grow.","title":"Planned tutorials and interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Cognitive-models-for-tutorials","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Models of metacognition, such as HMeta-d.\nThe models of hBayesDM, probably every one of them.\nThe main models for the Iowa Gambling Task, such as PVL-Delta and ORL.\nDifferent variants of the Rescorla-Wagner model, and models like it.\nEvidence accumulation models, such as attention-based models.\nBayesian models made with RxInfer.jl.\nActive inference models, which a separate package is being made for.\nAn example of a cognitive model which uses Turing.jl to select its actions.","title":"Cognitive models for tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"BayesianRegressionModels.jl.\nSimulation-based inference, where a neural network is trained on simulations to estimate the parameters: NeuralEstimators.jl, to test or to make an interface with, and perhaps the Python package sbi too. A simulation of a cognitive model is what both need.\nStanBlocks.jl, potentially, as another way to write the probabilistic model around a session.","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Fitting","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Dyadic interaction, with another agent as the environment of the agent that is fitted. When the other agent's actions are recorded, each agent is a session of its own whose observations are the other's actions. When they aren't, the other agent is the environment that fills them in.\nSession identifiers in the names of the variables of a fitted session, instead of session[i], if a way is found that doesn't compile the session once per identifier.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Plots","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Plots of a simulation, a history or a fitted session over time. The entry before the first timestep is timestep 0, at time 0, and its size is BeforeFirstTimestep(). The time of a later timestep is the sum of the sizes of the timesteps up to it.","title":"Plots"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"The functions and types that are only used inside the package are on the page Internals.\n\nPages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public","page":"API","text":"","title":"Public"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Defining-models","page":"API","text":"These are what a cognitive model or an environment model is written with, since the two are defined in the same way. They are public, but not exported, so use them with the package's name, as in CognitiveModeling.apply_model.","title":"Defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-a-model-must-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that a model must implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-a-model-can-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that a model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Fitting-several-sessions","page":"API","text":"These are what the parts of turing_full_model are written with, beside the types on the public list. They are public, but not exported.","title":"Fitting several sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Display","page":"API","text":"","title":"Display"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.CognitiveModeling","page":"API","text":"Computational modeling of cognition and behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CognitiveModeling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractCognitiveModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models, whose inputs are observations and whose outputs are actions.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add methods of apply_model, initialize_state and attribute_names for it. A model can also add methods of prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters, stopping_criterion, turing_apply_model and turing_initialize_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractEnvironmentModel\n\nSupertype for environment models, whose inputs are the actions of a cognitive model and whose outputs are its observations.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add the same methods as for an AbstractCognitiveModel: apply_model, initialize_state and attribute_names, and optionally prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters, stopping_criterion, turing_apply_model and turing_initialize_state.\n\nSee also simulate, which has an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue","page":"API","text":"AbstractNoValue\n\nSupertype for what stands in for an observation or an action that isn't there: NoObservation, NoAction and BeforeFirstTimestep. A model which treats them all the same can check for it, as in context.input.reward isa AbstractNoValue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts","page":"API","text":"AgentStarts()\n\nMake the agent move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the cognitive model updates and acts on the previous observation, and the environment then responds to its action. On the first timestep, the cognitive model receives an observation with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext, so that a cognitive model can act before it updates when that is what it means.\n\nSee also EnvironmentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep","page":"API","text":"BeforeFirstTimestep()\n\nStand-in for an observation or an action from before the first timestep, when there is none yet, as one entry of a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep()).\n\nA model receives it for every previous output on its first timestep, and for every input too when the other side hasn't moved yet: a cognitive model when the agent starts, and an environment model when the environment starts. NoObservation and NoAction are different: they say that nothing was observed or done on a timestep that did run.\n\nBefore the first timestep is timestep 0, which a simulation, a history and a fitted session start with when their SaveHistory saves the initial values, as it does by default. Its observation and action are this marker unless others are given, and so is its size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.Deterministic","page":"API","text":"Deterministic(value)\n\nAn action or an observation that is always value, for a cognitive model or an environment model which gives it without any randomness, as in (; reward = Deterministic(1.0)).\n\nA simulation samples every action and every observation with rand, and sampling from a Deterministic gives value itself. The value is kept in the field value.\n\nA simulation throws an error for a number, a string or a symbol that isn't wrapped, since none of them is something to sample from. A vector, a tuple or a range that isn't wrapped is sampled from as it is, which is rarely what is meant: rand picks one of its elements, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random: Xoshiro\n\njulia> rand(Xoshiro(1), Deterministic(1.0))\n1.0\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(true))).output_distribution.choice.value\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Deterministic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts","page":"API","text":"EnvironmentStarts()\n\nMake the environment move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the environment gives an observation in response to the previous action, and the cognitive model then updates and acts on it. On the first timestep, the environment receives an action with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext.\n\nSee also AgentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError","page":"API","text":"InvalidParametersError(message)\nInvalidParametersError()\n\nAn error for a model to throw from apply_model or initialize_state when its parameters aren't valid, as in throw(InvalidParametersError()).\n\nThe default message says that the parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on the observations, actions and states, and a model can give a more specific one. A simulation stops with it as with any other error, and fitting can catch it to give the parameters a probability of zero, so a model should throw this rather than an ordinary error when the parameters are the problem.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try\n           throw(InvalidParametersError())\n       catch error\n           sprint(showerror, error)\n       end\n\"InvalidParametersError: Model parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on observations, actions and states.\"\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, InvalidParametersError(\"The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"))\n\"InvalidParametersError: The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes","page":"API","text":"ModelAttributes(; parameters, states, inputs, outputs)\n\nThe names of the attributes of a model, which attribute_names returns.\n\nFor a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives.\n\nEach argument is a Tuple of Symbols, which is kept in the type, so that the names are known at compile time, and read as a property of the same name, such as attributes.parameters. A single Symbol is put in a tuple, and a Vector of them is made into one. All four must be given, and () means that the model has none of that kind. A name can only be used once across the four kinds, so that it says which attribute of the model is meant wherever the attributes are named together, and an ArgumentError is thrown otherwise.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> attributes = ModelAttributes(;\n           parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n           states = :expected_reward,\n           inputs = [:reward],\n           outputs = (),\n       )\nModelAttributes\nParameters: learning_rate, inverse_temperature\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> attributes.states\n(:expected_reward,)\n\njulia> ModelAttributes(; parameters = :choice, states = (), inputs = (), outputs = :choice)\nERROR: ArgumentError: The name choice is used for both a parameter and an output, but a model can only use a name for one attribute.\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelContext","page":"API","text":"ModelContext(input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n             rng=default_rng(), who_starts=UnknownWhoStarts())\n\nEverything a model needs to run a single timestep, which apply_model receives.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as context.input. For a cognitive model the input is its observation and the output its action, and for an environment model the input is the action of the cognitive model and the output the observation it gives.\n\nArguments\n\ninput: what the model receives on this timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the other side gave the entries, as in context.input.reward. An entry that isn't there on this timestep is NoObservation or NoAction. On the first timestep, when the other side hasn't moved yet, every entry is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nprevious_state: the state of the model after the previous timestep. On the first timestep, this is the output of initialize_state.\nprevious_output: what the model gave on the previous timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave the entries, as in context.previous_output.choice. On the first timestep, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nparameters: the parameters of the model, in a NamedTuple.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from if anything in the model is random, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\nwho_starts=UnknownWhoStarts(): who moves first on the timestep, AgentStarts or EnvironmentStarts as the simulation or the fitted session was told, or UnknownWhoStarts when neither is known. A model which can update before it acts or act before it updates can choose by it: for a cognitive model, with EnvironmentStarts() the input is this timestep's observation, and with AgentStarts() the previous timestep's. It is kept in the type, so that choosing costs nothing.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> input = (; reward = 1.0);\n\njulia> state = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1);\n\njulia> previous_output = (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep());\n\njulia> ModelContext(input, state, previous_output, parameters).timestep_size\n1\n\njulia> ModelContext(input, state, previous_output, parameters, 0.5).timestep_size\n0.5\n\njulia> ModelContext(input, state, previous_output, parameters).who_starts\nUnknownWhoStarts()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelContext"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput","page":"API","text":"ModelOutput(output_distribution, new_state=(;))\n\nThe outputs of a model for a single timestep, which apply_model returns.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as output.new_state.\n\nArguments\n\noutput_distribution::NamedTuple: what the model's outputs on this timestep are sampled from, in a NamedTuple with one entry for each output, such as (; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)) for the action of a cognitive model, or (; reward = Bernoulli(0.8)) for the observation an environment model gives. Each entry can be anything that works with rand, such as a distribution from Distributions.jl, or a Deterministic for an output that isn't random, and the sampled outputs keep the names. An output that isn't given on this timestep is NoAction for a cognitive model and NoObservation for an environment model, and a model without outputs gives an empty NamedTuple, (;).\nnew_state=(;): the state of the model after this timestep, which is the previous_state of the next one. The outputs are sampled after it is made, so it can't depend on them: the model receives them as context.previous_output on the next timestep.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Bernoulli\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)), (; expected_reward = 0.5)).new_state\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7))).new_state\nNamedTuple()\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = NoAction(), bet = Bernoulli(0.2))).output_distribution.choice\nNoAction()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoAction","page":"API","text":"NoAction()\n\nStand-in for an action that isn't taken on a timestep, as one entry of the actions NamedTuple, such as (; choice = NoAction()). Before the first timestep, an action is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoAction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoObservation","page":"API","text":"NoObservation()\n\nStand-in for an observation that isn't made on a timestep, as one entry of the observations NamedTuple, such as (; reward = NoObservation()). Before the first timestep, an observation is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoObservation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts","page":"API","text":"UnknownWhoStarts()\n\nThe who_starts of a ModelContext when neither side is known to move first on a timestep, as when a model runs on a single input with simulate, or is used outside a simulation. AgentStarts and EnvironmentStarts are the other two, which a simulation or a fitted session passes on from its own who_starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment","page":"API","text":"CommonRLEnvironment(rl_env; action_name)\n\nAn environment model around rl_env, an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl, which needs CommonRLInterface.jl to be loaded. action_name is the name of the cognitive model's action that act! receives, such as :arm.\n\nEnvironments from POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl can be used through it, with convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, x), which POMDPTools.jl defines for a POMDP or MDP and ReinforcementLearningBase.jl for its environments.\n\nrl_env itself is the state: initialize_state resets it with CommonRLInterface.jl's reset! and returns it, every timestep changes it in place with act! and passes it on as the new state, and prepare_state_for_saving copies it, with clone if it provides it and with deepcopy otherwise. A method of prepare_state_for_saving for a CommonRLEnvironment with a specific type of rl_env can save something lighter. The parameters aren't used, and neither is the random number generator, since an environment of CommonRLInterface.jl has its own randomness.\n\nEach observation is a NamedTuple (; observation, reward, terminated): what observe gives, the reward that act! returned, and whether the episode has ended. They are given as they are, each in a Deterministic, so nothing more is sampled. Before the first action, when the action is BeforeFirstTimestep, the environment is at its initial observation, so the reward is NoObservation and nothing is done with the action. The same goes for a timestep where the model takes no action, NoAction: the environment stays where it is. Its attribute_names are these three observations, the one action, no parameters and no states, since rl_env isn't a NamedTuple. Use EnvironmentStarts, so that the model observes before it acts.\n\nA simulation stops by itself when the episode ends, since the stopping_criterion of the environment is terminated of rl_env, so n_steps is the most timesteps it can take. An episode that has ended isn't started again, and an action given to it throws an error. A new call of simulate starts a new episode, and so does reset! of an InstantiatedModel, since both reset rl_env through initialize_state.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing CommonRLInterface\n\n#A two-armed bandit where the first arm always pays, in episodes of two pulls\nmutable struct Bandit <: CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv\n    pulls::Int\nend\nCommonRLInterface.reset!(rl_env::Bandit) = (rl_env.pulls = 0; nothing)\nCommonRLInterface.actions(rl_env::Bandit) = 1:2\nCommonRLInterface.observe(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls\nCommonRLInterface.act!(rl_env::Bandit, arm) = (rl_env.pulls += 1; arm == 1 ? 1.0 : 0.0)\nCommonRLInterface.terminated(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls == 2\n\n#A model which always pulls the first arm\nstruct PullsFirstArm <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::PullsFirstArm, parameters) = (;)\nCognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::PullsFirstArm, context::ModelContext) = ModelOutput((; arm = Deterministic(1)))\nCognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::PullsFirstArm) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = (:observation, :reward, :terminated), outputs = :arm)\n\n#The simulation stops when the episode ends, before it has run all of n_steps\nsimulation = simulate(\n    PullsFirstArm(),\n    CommonRLEnvironment(Bandit(0); action_name = :arm);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 10,\n    cognitive_parameters = (;),\n    environment_parameters = (;),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#Timestep 0, before the first timestep, and then what the environment gave on each timestep\nforeach(println, simulation.observations)\n\n# output\n\n(observation = BeforeFirstTimestep(), reward = BeforeFirstTimestep(), terminated = BeforeFirstTimestep())\n(observation = 0, reward = NoObservation(), terminated = false)\n(observation = 1, reward = 1.0, terminated = false)\n(observation = 2, reward = 1.0, terminated = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything","page":"API","text":"SaveEverything()\n\nMarker for saving every attribute of a kind, used in SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory","page":"API","text":"SaveHistory(; states=(), inputs=(), outputs=(), output_distributions=(),\n            save_initial_values=true)\nSaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_values=true)\n\nWhich states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of a model a simulation or a fitted session saves, by name, and whether it saves them from before the first timestep too.\n\nstates, inputs, outputs and output_distributions are each the names of that kind to save, in a Tuple of Symbols, a single Symbol or a Vector of them, or SaveEverything for all of them. The names are the model's own, as attribute_names lists them: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. The output distributions are what the model gave its outputs to be sampled from, named like the outputs. A kind that isn't given saves nothing, so SaveHistory() saves nothing, which is the default everywhere, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. The selections are kept in the type, so that selecting costs nothing in a simulation, and are read as properties, such as save_history.states.\n\nsave_initial_values is whether what is saved starts with timestep 0, before the first timestep: the initial states, and the previous input and output, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given. It is true by default, which matters when the initial state depends on parameters that are fitted. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 of what is saved is then timestep t, and with false, element t is.\n\nSaving some names of a kind gives a NamedTuple with those names on each timestep, which only works when the value is a NamedTuple, and saving none gives an empty one, (;), for any value. Before the first timestep there is no output distribution, so timestep 0 has BeforeFirstTimestep() for each name that is saved.\n\nEach model of a simulation has one: simulate and SessionInfo take cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history, and an InstantiatedModel takes save_history. What a simulation or a session returns names the saved values by their role: the cognitive model's inputs as observations and outputs as actions, and the environment's inputs as actions and outputs as observations under environment, as simulate describes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice, states = (:expected_reward, :surprise))\nSaveHistory\nStates: expected_reward, surprise\nInputs: none\nOutputs: choice\nOutput distributions: none\nInitial values: saved\n\njulia> save_history.states\n(:expected_reward, :surprise)\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything()).inputs\nSaveEverything()\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_values = false).save_initial_values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel","page":"API","text":"InstantiatedModel(model, parameters; save_history=SaveHistory())\nInstantiatedModel(model, parameters, state, previous_output; save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nA cognitive model or an environment model with its parameters, current state and previous output, which simulate! can update iteratively.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as agent.state. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them. The state defaults to the output of initialize_state with the full parameters, and the previous output to every output being BeforeFirstTimestep. The model must have a method of attribute_names, and the names of the parameters, of the previous output, and of the state when it is a NamedTuple, must be those it lists, or an error is thrown.\n\nagent.history holds a ModelHistory with where the model started, which is timestep 0, and everything that happens to it, as selected by save_history, a SaveHistory. By default nothing is selected and the vectors in agent.history stay empty, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. Inputs and outputs can only be selected by name among those the model lists, since the history starts from those names.\n\nThe same type serves as the agent and as the environment of a simulation, since both are models. See also simulate! and reset!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory","page":"API","text":"ModelHistory(states, inputs, outputs, output_distributions, timestep_sizes)\n\nWhat has happened to an InstantiatedModel, which simulate! adds to.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as history.states. The names are the model's own: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it received and the outputs the observations it gave. The model's SaveHistory selects what is kept of each kind, and when it selects nothing at all, the vectors stay empty. Each vector starts with timestep 0, where the model started, when the SaveHistory saves the initial values, which it does by default, so that element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\nArguments\n\nstates::Vector{Any}: the states of the model, starting at timestep 0 with its initial state. Later states are those after each timestep. All of them are as returned by prepare_state_for_saving.\ninputs::Vector{Any}: what the model received on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with every input the model lists as BeforeFirstTimestep, since it has received nothing before the simulation.\noutputs::Vector{Any}: what the model gave on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with its previous output, which by default has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep().\noutput_distributions::Vector{Any}: what the model gave each output to be sampled from, starting at timestep 0 with BeforeFirstTimestep() for every output, since nothing was given yet.\ntimestep_sizes::Vector{Any}: how much time each timestep covered, starting with BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0, so that it has an entry for each entry of the other vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NameMap","page":"API","text":"NameMap(names...)\n\nWhich outputs of one side of a simulation the other side receives, and under which names.\n\nEach argument is :given => :received, for an output which the receiver lists under another name, or a single Symbol, for one which keeps its name. Outputs that aren't in the map aren't passed on, and each name can only be there once. The names are kept in the type, so that a map costs nothing in a simulation.\n\nsimulate and simulate! take one as observation_map, from the observations the environment model gives to those the cognitive model lists, and one as action_map, from the actions the cognitive model gives to those the environment model lists. A cognitive model and an environment which name things differently, or where one gives something the other doesn't list, can then be used together, and the map says which outputs the receiver gets: it must give exactly the names the receiver lists in attribute_names. NameMap() passes nothing on, for a receiver which lists nothing. Without a map, the two must list the same names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\nNameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue => :reward)\nERROR: ArgumentError: A NameMap can only give each name once, but it gives (:reward, :reward).\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NameMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.reset!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel}","page":"API","text":"reset!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel)\n\nPut instantiated_model back to where a new one starts, and return it.\n\nThe state becomes the output of initialize_state with the parameters it has, and the previous output has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep again. The history is a new one which starts from there, as timestep 0 when the SaveHistory saves the initial values, so a history that was read before the reset, as in history = agent.history, is kept as it was. The model, the parameters and the SaveHistory stay the same.\n\nAn instantiated model that was created with a state or a previous output of its own doesn't return to those. Create a new one for that.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nreset!(agent)\n\nagent.state, agent.previous_output, agent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n((expected_reward = 0.5,), (choice = BeforeFirstTimestep(),), Any[(expected_reward = 0.5,)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"simulate!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel, input; timestep_size=1,\n          rng=default_rng())\n\nRun a single timestep of instantiated_model on input, update it, and return the sampled output. What instantiated_model.save_history selects of the state, the input, the output and the output distribution is added to instantiated_model.history, together with the size of the timestep.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice, as for simulate. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input the model lists in attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown.\n\nArguments\n\ninstantiated_model::InstantiatedModel: the model to update.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoObservation or NoAction.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\n\nagent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Any}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, InstantiatedModel}","page":"API","text":"simulate!(agent::InstantiatedModel, environment::InstantiatedModel; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate agent interacting with environment from where they are, update them both, and return the same NamedTuple as simulate, without timestep 0: what agent.save_history selects of the states, observations, actions and action distributions on each timestep of this call, what environment.save_history selects of the environment's under environment, and the sizes of the timesteps. What the two select is also added to agent.history and environment.history, in each model's own names, together with the sizes of the timesteps, unless a selection is empty. A history has timestep 0 from where the model started, when its SaveHistory saves the initial values.\n\nThe two models must fit together, as for simulate, directly or through observation_map and action_map. The agent's history holds the observations as its model received them, and the environment's history the actions as its model did.\n\nThe simulation ends before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false. A model where it is met already can't be simulated further, and throws an error until it is reset!.\n\nReading the untyped fields of the instantiated models costs a dynamic dispatch on each call, which makes the return type unknown to the compiler. The loop inside is still fully typed. Use simulate if the return type matters, or if most calls are for a single timestep.\n\nArguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep of this call. A vector has one size for each timestep of this call, so its length must be n_steps.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of the environment the agent receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of the agent the environment receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep, as for simulate.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RandomChoices <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RandomChoices, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RandomChoices, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.5)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RandomChoices)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = :timestep, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nstruct TimestepCounter <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TimestepCounter, parameters)\n    return (; n_timesteps = 0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TimestepCounter, context::ModelContext)\n    n_timesteps = context.previous_state.n_timesteps + 1\n    return ModelOutput((; timestep = Deterministic(n_timesteps)), (; n_timesteps))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TimestepCounter)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :choice, outputs = :timestep)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RandomChoices(), (;))\nenvironment = InstantiatedModel(TimestepCounter(), (;))\n\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 2)\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 3)\n\nenvironment.state\n\n# output\n\n(n_timesteps = 5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"simulate(model, input; <keyword arguments>)\n\nRun a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the sampled output and the new state of the model.\n\nNothing is kept between calls, so pass the returned state and output on as previous_state and previous_output to run the next timestep. simulate! on a single input does the same for an InstantiatedModel, which keeps them. The compiler knows the types here, which it doesn't for an instantiated model, so this is the faster of the two.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input the model lists in attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if model provides defaults for them, and the names of the parameters, the state and the previous output are checked as in a full simulation.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::AbstractModel: the model.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoObservation or NoAction.\nparameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nprevious_state=initialize_state(model, ...): the state of model before this timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_output=initial_output(model): the output before this timestep. By default every output is BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5)\n\n#The first timestep starts from the initial state\naction, state = simulate(RescorlaWagner(), (; reward = 1.0); parameters)\n\n#The next one continues from what the first returned\naction, state = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = 1.0);\n    parameters,\n    previous_state = state,\n    previous_output = action,\n)\n\nstate\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"API","text":"simulate(cognitive_model, environment; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate cognitive_model interacting with environment, and return what was saved in a NamedTuple: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep after it.\n\nEach element is what happened on that timestep, and the states are those after it. The observations are those the cognitive model received, and the actions those it gave, and under environment the observations are those the environment gave and the actions those it received, which differ from the cognitive model's when a NameMap selects or renames them. The action distributions are what the cognitive model gave its actions to be sampled from, and the observation distributions what the environment gave its observations to be sampled from. The element for timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the states before the first timestep. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\ncognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select what is kept of each element of each model, which is nothing by default: give SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) to keep everything, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_values = false) to leave out timestep 0, so that element t is timestep t. The vectors of both models have the same length, which cognitive_save_history decides. The sizes of the timesteps are returned as they were given: a single size as it is, and a vector of sizes with the sizes of the timesteps that ran, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there.\n\nParameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if cognitive_model or environment provides defaults for them. The names of the parameters, the states, the previous action and the previous observation must be those in attribute_names, and the outputs of each model must be the inputs of the other, or an ArgumentError is thrown before the first timestep. When the two name things differently, or one gives something the other doesn't list, observation_map and action_map say which outputs of one the other receives, and under which names.\n\nThe simulation ends before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false, and everything returned then has the timesteps that ran.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nenvironment::AbstractModel: the environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel. A cognitive model can serve as the environment of another when its outputs are what the other lists as its inputs, and the other way round.\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, which both cognitive_model and environment receive. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep. A vector has one size for each timestep, so its length must be n_steps.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of cognitive_model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of environment, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample actions and observations from, which cognitive_model and environment also receive in their contexts.\nprevious_cognitive_state=initialize_state(cognitive_model, ...): the state of cognitive_model before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that cognitive_model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_action=initial_output(cognitive_model): the action before the first timestep. By default every action is BeforeFirstTimestep, with the names from attribute_names.\nprevious_environment_state=initialize_state(environment, ...): the state of environment before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters.\nprevious_observation=initial_output(environment): the observation before the first timestep. By default every observation is BeforeFirstTimestep(), with the names from attribute_names of environment.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model are saved, by name, and whether timestep 0 is. Nothing is saved by default, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. See SaveHistory.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions it receives and whose outputs are the observations it gives. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history, so that the vectors of both models have the same length.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment are passed on to cognitive_model, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names environment gives to exactly those cognitive_model lists. Without one, the two must list the same observations, which cognitive_model receives under the same names.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of cognitive_model are passed on to environment, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names cognitive_model gives to exactly those environment lists. Without one, the two must list the same actions, which environment receives under the same names.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep. With false, all of n_steps timesteps run.\n\nSee also simulate!, which continues from where the last simulation ended.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli, BernoulliLogit\n\n#A cognitive model which learns the expected reward\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\n#An environment which takes the choice, and gives a reward with a fixed probability whatever it is\nstruct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::BernoulliRewards, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(environment::BernoulliRewards, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; reward = Bernoulli(context.parameters.reward_probability)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :reward_probability, states = (), inputs = :choice, outputs = :reward)\nend\n\nsimulation = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    BernoulliRewards();\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 3,\n    cognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5),\n    environment_parameters = (; reward_probability = 1.0),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#The initial state, as timestep 0, and the state after each timestep\nsimulation.states\n\n# output\n\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{expected_reward::Float64}}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n (expected_reward = 0.9375,)\n\nExtended help\n\nThe return type is concrete as long as the types of the outputs don't change between timesteps. If they do, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoObservation, the vector for that output holds a Union of the types, and the simulation is a little slower. The same goes for timestep 0 when it has other types than the timesteps after it, which the observations and actions usually do, since they are BeforeFirstTimestep() there.\n\nNothing is copied when outputs are saved. A model or environment which changes an object in its state in place, instead of returning a new one, must add a method of prepare_state_for_saving, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. Observations and actions have no such function, so they must be new objects on each timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractDownstreamModel\n\nSupertype for the descriptions of what runs after the sessions in a turing_full_model, with what they saved, such as a regression of a measurement on a state. NoDownstreamModel is the default.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add a method of turing_downstream_model for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractSessionParametersModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractSessionParametersModel\n\nSupertype for the descriptions of how the parameters of every session are given or sampled in a turing_full_model, for the cognitive model and for the environment model alike. The package comes with NoParameters, FixedSessionParameters, FixedParametersBySession, IdenticalSessionParameters and IndependentSessionParameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add a method of turing_session_parameters_model for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractSessionParametersModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.FixedParametersBySession","page":"API","text":"FixedParametersBySession(parameters_by_session)\n\nThe parameters model of a turing_full_model which gives each session values of its own, with nothing sampled. parameters_by_session is a NamedTuple with an entry for each session, named by the session's identifier, with the values of that session, such as (; S1 = (; scale = 2.0), S2 = (; scale = 3.0)). An identifier from several session columns is named by its values joined with underscores, as in S1_2. Every session must have values for the same parameters. The values are kept in the field parameters_by_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedParametersBySession"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.FixedSessionParameters","page":"API","text":"FixedSessionParameters(parameters)\n\nThe parameters model of a turing_full_model which gives every session the same values, such as (; scale = 2.0), with nothing sampled. The values are kept in the field parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.IdenticalSessionParameters","page":"API","text":"IdenticalSessionParameters(priors)\n\nThe parameters model of a turing_full_model which samples each parameter once, from its prior, and gives every session that value. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2)). In the Turing.jl model, each parameter is named after itself, such as learning_rate.shared. The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IdenticalSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.IndependentSessionParameters","page":"API","text":"IndependentSessionParameters(priors)\n\nThe parameters model of a turing_full_model which samples each parameter for every session from the same prior, with nothing shared between the sessions. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2), inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1)). In the Turing.jl model, each parameter is a vector with one entry for each session, named after the parameter, such as learning_rate.session, so the learning rate of the third session is learning_rate.session[3]. The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IndependentSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.MissingValue","page":"API","text":"MissingValue()\n\nStand-in for an action or an observation that happened on a timestep but isn't in the data, as one entry of a column or a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = MissingValue()). Whether it is an action or an observation is given by where it is.\n\nFitting imputes a missing action by sampling it from the model's action distribution on that timestep, or passes it on as NoAction when it is told not to impute. It imputes a missing observation from what an environment model gives for the observation on that timestep: as a latent variable with that distribution, or as the value of a Deterministic. NoAction and NoObservation are different: they say that nothing was done or observed on the timestep. Unlike missing, arithmetic on it is an error. A SessionInfo replaces missing in the data by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MissingValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo","page":"API","text":"MultipleSessionsInfo(cognitive_model, table; observation_columns, action_columns,\n                     session_columns, <keyword arguments>)\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions, session_ids)\n\nThe sessions that a cognitive model is fitted to together, as a vector of SessionInfo with an identifier for each, which turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions and turing_full_model take.\n\nThe first form splits a table into sessions. table is a NamedTuple with one vector for each column, which is what Tables.columntable gives for a DataFrame or any other table. A missing in a column becomes MissingValue, or the marker for nothing happening, as the keyword arguments missing_observations and missing_actions of SessionInfo say. Every session must come out with the same type, which they do when they share the models and the settings, since the timesteps of different sessions are then compiled once. An environment runs only in the sessions where an observation or an action is missing, as for SessionInfo.\n\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nThe same sessions with other save histories for every one, see SessionInfo.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\ntable::NamedTuple: the data of every session, with one vector for each column and one entry for each timestep, such as (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0], choice = [true, false, true, true]).\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nobservation_columns: the columns with the observations. When the model lists one observation, a single name, such as :outcome, is its column, whatever the observation is called. Several observations are given as the names the model lists in attribute_names when the columns are named the same, such as (:reward, :cue), or as a NamedTuple from the model's name to the column's, such as (; reward = :outcome, cue = :stimulus), so that one model fits tables with other names. A single name that the model lists as one of its actions is an error, since the two keyword arguments may then be swapped. An attribute whose value is made of several columns is specified by how to build it from them: a NamedTuple of column names for a NamedTuple in each row, (; ssm_action = (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol)), a Tuple of column names for a Tuple, (; ssm_action = (:choicecol, :rtcol)), or a Tuple of column names and a function of their values, (; ssm_action = (:choicecol, :rtcol) => (choice, rt) -> [choice, rt]). A missing in any of the columns makes the value missing.\naction_columns: the columns with the actions, in the same forms.\nsession_columns: the column or columns which identify a session, such as :id or (:id, :block). A session is every row with one combination of their values, in the order of the first row of each. The identifier of a session is its value in a single column, or a NamedTuple of its values in several.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as one size for all, or the name of a column with the size of each timestep.\nThe other keyword arguments of SessionInfo, which every session gets.\n\nThe second form takes sessions that are already prepared. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> table = (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], outcome = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0], choice = [true, false, true, missing, false]);\n\njulia> sessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(Counter(), table; observation_columns = (; reward = :outcome), action_columns = :choice, session_columns = :id, who_starts = EnvironmentStarts())\nMultipleSessionsInfo\nSessions: 2\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\n\njulia> sessions_info.session_ids\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"S1\"\n \"S2\"\n\njulia> sessions_info.sessions[2].actions\n(choice = Union{MissingValue, Bool}[true, MissingValue(), false],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel","page":"API","text":"NoDownstreamModel()\n\nThe downstream_model of a turing_full_model that has no third part: nothing is sampled after the sessions, and the downstream_values the model returns are nothing. This is the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment","page":"API","text":"NoEnvironment()\n\nThe environment of a session that has none, which is the default of SessionInfo. Every observation then comes from the data, and none can be MissingValue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoParallelisation","page":"API","text":"NoParallelisation()\n\nThe default parallelisation of turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions and turing_full_model: the sessions run one after the other. See ParallelSessions for the other choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoParallelisation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoParameters","page":"API","text":"NoParameters()\n\nThe parameters model of a turing_full_model which gives no parameters, so that each parameter takes the value the model gives it in default_parameters. It is the default for the parameters of the environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions","page":"API","text":"ParallelSessions()\n\nGiven as parallelisation to turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions or turing_full_model, it spreads the sessions over the threads Julia was started with, as Threads.@threads does. The model that is evaluated, which is the outermost one, must then be marked with DynamicPPL's setthreadsafe(model, true), and the sessions throw an error if it isn't, since the log density is silently wrong otherwise. The gradient of a threaded model is right with ForwardDiff and ReverseDiff, and Mooncake and Enzyme throw. With 4 threads and 20 sessions of 200 timesteps, the log density took 26 µs instead of 39, and a ForwardDiff gradient 244 µs instead of 471.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo","page":"API","text":"SessionInfo(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nEverything about one session that is fixed while a cognitive model is fitted to it: the data, the models and the settings, checked and prepared once. A Turing.jl model of the session, turing_behavioural_session, takes it together with the parameters, which are what changes between evaluations. MultipleSessionsInfo makes one for each session in a table.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nobservations: the observations in the data, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each name and one entry for each timestep, such as (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]). The data must have every observation the model lists in attribute_names, and can have more. When the model lists one observation, a single vector, [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], is the data of that observation, whatever its name. An observation that wasn't recorded is MissingValue, which environment fills in, and one that wasn't made is NoObservation.\nactions: the actions in the data, in the same forms, with exactly the actions the model lists. An action that was taken but not recorded is MissingValue(), and is imputed unless impute_missing_actions is false. An action that wasn't taken is NoAction, and isn't scored.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: whether the model acts on the observation of the same timestep, EnvironmentStarts(), or on the observation of the timestep before, AgentStarts(), as in simulate. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nenvironment::Union{NoEnvironment, AbstractModel}=NoEnvironment(): an environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel, which fills in the observations that are MissingValue(), and against whose distributions the observations in the data are scored. Its outputs must be the inputs of cognitive_model and the other way round, since a session takes no maps of names. Without it, the data can't have missing observations. It runs in a session where an observation or an action is missing, where environment_save_history saves anything, or where check_stopping_criterion is true: the scored observations then inform what is missing, such as which arm of a bandit a missing choice was, from the reward that followed. A session with nothing missing runs without it, since the scores of its observations would then be the same for every value of the parameters, unless the environment's parameters are sampled, see turing_full_model. The session then has none of the environment's variables either. The environment is kept in the session all the same.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as in simulate.\nimpute_missing_actions::Bool=true: whether an action that is MissingValue() is imputed, as a latent variable. When it is false, nothing is sampled for it, and it is replaced by NoAction() here: cognitive_model receives NoAction() as that previous action on the next timestep, and environment as the action it responds to.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model a fitted session returns, by name, and whether timestep 0 is, as a SaveHistory in the model's own names. Nothing is selected by default. What is returned names them by their role, as states, observations, actions and action_distributions, see turing_behavioural_session.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions and whose outputs are the observations, returned under environment. Selecting anything makes the environment run in the session, also when nothing is missing, which adds its scores of the observations to the log density; with fixed environment parameters they are the same for every value of the cognitive parameters, so the posterior is unchanged. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Union{Nothing, Bool}=nothing: when stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before a timestep, so that the session ends there, with what it saved up to it. By default it is checked only on the timesteps after the last one with data, where a value is neither MissingValue() nor a marker for nothing happening, so that a fit never checks it and a session made by predictive_sessions, whose data is all missing, ends as a simulation does. With true it is checked before every timestep, which makes the environment run, and a model whose criterion is met while the data continues is an error, since the recorded values after that timestep couldn't be scored: this checks that the models agree with the length of the data. With false it is never checked.\nmissing_observations::Union{MissingValue, NoObservation}=MissingValue() and missing_actions::Union{MissingValue, NoAction}=MissingValue(): what a missing in the data becomes, since a table marks every gap the same way: a value that wasn't recorded, or nothing happening on that timestep. The two markers can also be put in the data directly.\nprevious_observation and previous_action: what the first timestep receives as the observation and the action before it, with BeforeFirstTimestep for each name by default, as in simulate. The states where the session starts are not given here, since they come from initialize_state with the parameters that are fitted.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with the two replacements made in the data, except impute_missing_actions, whose work is done here. There are five fields more: runs_environment, whether the environment runs, recorded_actions and recorded_observations, the recorded values of each name which are scored, n_timesteps, saved_timestep_sizes, the sizes a fitted session returns with what it saves, and first_timestep_to_check_stopping, the first timestep before which the stopping criterion is checked, as check_stopping_criterion decides it.\n\nSessionInfo(session_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nThe same session with other save histories, for reading the states of a fitted session from a chain: a session is fitted fastest when it saves nothing, and a chain from that model gives the states through returned with the model of a copy that saves them, since the two have the same variables. See turing_full_model.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, missing]), (; choice = [true, missing, false]); who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), missing_observations = NoObservation())\nSessionInfo\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nTimesteps: 3\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\nWho starts: environment\n\njulia> session_info.observations.reward[3], session_info.actions.choice[2]\n(NoObservation(), MissingValue())\n\njulia> session_info.recorded_actions\n(choice = Bool[1, 0],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.predictive_sessions-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"API","text":"predictive_sessions(session_info)\npredictive_sessions(sessions_info)\n\nReturn a copy of a SessionInfo, or of every session in a MultipleSessionsInfo, where every action is MissingValue, and every observation too when the session has an environment model, so that a Turing.jl model of the copy simulates them. This is for predictive checks: predict with the chain of a model fitted to the sessions, or sampling the model of the copy from its prior, simulates each session again, with the same number of timesteps at most, the same settings and the same environment. The observations of a session without an environment stay those in the data, as for an experiment whose observations are fixed in advance. Every missing action is imputed, whatever impute_missing_actions the session was prepared with. Since no timestep of the copy has data, the stopping_criterion of the models is checked before every timestep unless the session was prepared with check_stopping_criterion = false, so the copy can end before the data did, as a simulation does.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], [true, false, true]; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts());\n\njulia> predictive_sessions(session_info).actions\n(choice = [MissingValue(), MissingValue(), MissingValue()],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.predictive_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioural_session","page":"API","text":"turing_behavioural_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters)\nturing_behavioural_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters,\n                           environment_parameters=(;))\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model of one session: the cognitive model in session_info goes through the timesteps of its data with cognitive_parameters, and the recorded actions are scored against the action distributions it gives. The second form is for a session with an environment model, which takes its parameters too. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters, as a submodel with its own prefix: to_submodel(prefix(turing_behavioural_session(...), @varname(session)), false). turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions runs one for each of several sessions, and turing_full_model samples their parameters too.\n\nArguments\n\nsession_info::SessionInfo: the data, the models and the settings of the session, prepared once with SessionInfo.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of the cognitive model, usually the random variables of the model around this one. Those it doesn't have take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): the parameters of the environment model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any. A session without an environment takes none.\n\nWhat it does\n\nThe states where the session starts come from turing_initialize_state of each model with the parameters, so that a starting state which is a parameter is fitted like any other, and a model can sample its start. Both models are treated the same way on each timestep: turing_apply_model of the cognitive model runs as a submodel with the prefix timestep[t], so a model which samples within a timestep has its own variables for each timestep, such as timestep[3].noise, and an environment model runs with the prefix timestep[t].environment. The outputs of each, the actions and the observations, are then taken from the data where they are recorded, and where the data has MissingValue they are imputed from what the model gave: as a latent variable with that distribution, named timestep[3].choice.imputed or timestep[3].environment.reward.imputed, or as the value itself if it is a Deterministic. The sampler must handle the type of a latent variable: a continuous value can be sampled with NUTS, a discrete one needs a sampler for discrete variables. The recorded actions are scored at the end as observed values, in one product_distribution over every action and timestep, against the distributions the cognitive model gave for them, which is several times faster than scoring each timestep on its own. The recorded observations are scored the same way against the distributions the environment gave, so that they inform the missing values and the environment's parameters. A Deterministic is scored as a point mass, with a log density of 0 for its value and -Inf for any other, and only a number or a Bool can be, so a recorded value the model gives as another kind of Deterministic is an error. A recorded value where the model gave nothing, NoAction or NoObservation, is an error too. When nothing in the data is missing and the environment's history isn't saved, the environment doesn't run, and the session is the same as one without an environment, with the same variables. The stopping_criterion of the models is checked as the check_stopping_criterion of session_info says: by default only after the last timestep with data, so that a fit runs every timestep and a session from predictive_sessions can end early.\n\nWhat it returns\n\nWhat the save histories of session_info select, in a NamedTuple with the same entries as simulate returns: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment when the session has one, which are empty when it didn't run, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep that ran, which returned(model, chain) gives for each sample. Timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the initial states, which can depend on the parameters. Element t + 1 is then timestep t, and a SaveHistory with save_initial_values = false leaves timestep 0 out. The returned actions and observations are those the models received, with the imputed values. timestep_sizes has the size of each timestep, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there, when anything is selected, and is empty otherwise. Any latent variable in a session, imputed or from a model which samples within a timestep, makes evaluating the model much slower in the current version of DynamicPPL.jl.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The model to fit, a Rescorla-Wagner model with a softmax choice\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(context.parameters.inverse_temperature * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature, :initial_value),\n        states = :expected_reward,\n        inputs = :reward,\n        outputs = :choice,\n    )\nend\n\nCognitiveModeling.default_parameters(model::RescorlaWagner) = (; initial_value = 0.5)\n\n#The data of one session, where the third choice wasn't recorded, prepared once\nsession_info = SessionInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0]),\n    (; choice = [true, false, MissingValue(), true]);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Priors on the parameters, and the session as a submodel\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(0, 1)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        prefix(\n            turing_behavioural_session(session_info, (; learning_rate, inverse_temperature)),\n            @varname(session),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return session\nend\n\nchain = sample(fit_one_session(session_info), NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The expected rewards on each timestep, for each sample\ntrajectories = returned(fit_one_session(session_info), chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioural_session"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters","page":"API","text":"turing_catch_invalid_parameters(model)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs model, any Turing.jl model, inside a try block: an InvalidParametersError thrown by a cognitive model or an environment model then gives the parameters a log density of -Inf, so that a sampler rejects the sample instead of stopping, and the model returns nothing for that sample. Otherwise it returns what model returns. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nIt goes around the part of a model that can throw, with the parameters sampled outside it, as in\n\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        turing_catch_invalid_parameters(\n            turing_behavioural_session(session_info, (; learning_rate)),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\nend\n\nand turing_full_model does this with catch_invalid_parameters = true. The variables sampled inside the try block are lost when it catches an error, so around a whole model a sampler can't start when its first draw is rejected. Anything that uses what it returns after a rejection, which is nothing, can't be differentiated with Enzyme, so return or stop at once when it is nothing. Mooncake can't differentiate a model with a try block at all, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no warning. It works with ForwardDiff and with Enzyme. Nothing in the package catches the error unless this is used, so a sampler stops with it otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model","page":"API","text":"turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                  environment_parameters_model=NoParameters(),\n                  downstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(), catch_invalid_parameters=false,\n                  parallelisation=NoParallelisation(), include_submodel_prefixes=false)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model which fits a cognitive model to several sessions, in three parts: cognitive_parameters_model gives or samples the cognitive parameters of every session, and environment_parameters_model those of the environment model, the sessions in sessions_info run with them as turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions, and downstream_model can do something with what they saved, such as a regression of a measurement on a state. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_parameters_model: a description of how the cognitive parameters of the sessions are given or sampled, a subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel such as IndependentSessionParameters with a prior for each parameter, which turing_session_parameters_model has a method for.\nsessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo: the sessions, prepared once with MultipleSessionsInfo.\nenvironment_parameters_model=NoParameters(): the same for the parameters of the environment model of the sessions, when they have one. By default the environment's parameters take its default values, and FixedSessionParameters gives others. When they are sampled, every session runs its environment, also those with nothing missing, so that the observations in the data inform them. Sessions without an environment take no parameters.\ndownstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(): a description of what runs after the sessions, which turing_downstream_model has a method for. The default samples nothing.\ncatch_invalid_parameters::Bool=false: whether the sessions run inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, so that an InvalidParametersError rejects the sample instead of stopping the sampler. The parameters models run outside it, so that a rejected first draw keeps its parameters and the sampler can start, and the model returns nothing for a rejected sample, without running the downstream model. A downstream model with variables of its own then lacks them in a rejected draw, so a sampler can't start from one: give it valid initial_params in that case.\nparallelisation=NoParallelisation(): whether the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with, with ParallelSessions(). The model must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL before it is sampled: without the mark, a threaded model has a wrong log density with no error, so the sessions throw an error instead. The gradient is right with ForwardDiff and ReverseDiff, and Mooncake and Enzyme throw. See ParallelSessions.\ninclude_submodel_prefixes::Bool=false: whether the variables of the two parameters models and of the downstream model are named under cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model and downstream_model. Without the prefixes, the names are shorter, but a variable of the same name in two of them is one variable, which sample refuses with an error, and which a log density gives one value with both priors counted. A warning says so when the model is made, when the cognitive model and the environment model have a parameter of the same name and both parameters models sample.\n\nThe variables of a parameters model are named as it names them, such as learning_rate.session, a vector with a value for each session, for IndependentSessionParameters, and learning_rate.shared for IdenticalSessionParameters, or under the parameters model's name with include_submodel_prefixes = true. Those of the sessions are under session[i], and those of the downstream model as it names them, or under downstream_model with the prefixes. The model returns (; cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, saved_behavioural_values, downstream_values): the cognitive parameters and the environment parameters of every session as the parameters models gave them, what each session saved, as a vector of what turing_behavioural_session returns, and what the downstream model returned. returned(model, chain) gives all three for each sample, so a fitted state is read from the chain without simulating again.\n\nSaving states costs about 5 % of a gradient. Without a downstream model that needs them, a fit is fastest with sessions that save nothing, and the saved values are then read from the chain afterwards with session_values_chain, or with returned on the model of a copy of the sessions that saves them, MultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward)), since the two models have the same variables. A warning says so when the sessions save anything and there is no downstream model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The data of two sessions, as a table with one vector for each column\ntable = (;\n    id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"],\n    reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n    choice = [true, false, true, false, missing, true],\n)\n\n#The sessions, with the RescorlaWagner model of turing_behavioural_session\nsessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    table;\n    observation_columns = :reward,\n    action_columns = :choice,\n    session_columns = :id,\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Each session has its own parameters, from the same priors\ncognitive_parameters_model = IndependentSessionParameters((;\n    learning_rate = Beta(2, 2),\n    inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1),\n))\n\nmodel = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The parameters and the expected rewards of every session, for each sample\nresults = returned(model, chain)\nresults[1].cognitive_parameters.learning_rate\nresults[1].saved_behavioural_values[2].states\n\n#The same fit without saving, and the states read from the chain afterwards\nunsaved = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, MultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory()))\nchain = sample(unsaved, NUTS(), 1000)\nresults = returned(model, chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions","page":"API","text":"turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions(sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo,\n                                     cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters=(;);\n                                     parallelisation=NoParallelisation())\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs turing_behavioural_session for every session in sessions_info, each with its own cognitive parameters, as a submodel with the prefix session[i], so that a latent variable of the third session is named like session[3].timestep[2].choice.imputed. Which session i is stands in sessions_info.session_ids[i], and session_index gives i for an identifier. Environment parameters can only be given when the sessions have an environment model. With parallelisation = ParallelSessions(), the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with. The model that is evaluated must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL, since the log density of a threaded model that isn't is wrong with no error; the sessions throw an error when it isn't marked. See ParallelSessions. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\ncognitive_parameters is a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, with one value for every session in the order of sessions_info, which is what a parameters model returns, see turing_session_parameters_model, and environment_parameters is in the same form. It returns a vector with what each session returns, in the same order. The model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters of the sessions in some way of its own, as a submodel without a prefix: to_submodel(turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions(sessions_info, parameters), false). turing_full_model does this with parameters models.\n\nThe session identifiers aren't in the names of the variables because each identifier would give its sessions a prefix of its own type, and DynamicPPL compiles the code of a session once for each: with 40 sessions, the first evaluation took 1.5 s instead of 0.2, and every one after it 44 ns per timestep instead of 9.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.predict_session_values","page":"API","text":"predict_session_values([rng,] model, chain; cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                       environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nSimulate the sessions of model again with the parameters of every sample in chain, and return what they saved as a FlexiChain, as session_values_chain makes it. This is for prior and posterior predictive checks, and needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nmodel is made by turing_full_model, turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions or turing_behavioural_session, or is such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters. Its sessions are made into predictive_sessions, so that every action is simulated, and every observation too when the sessions have an environment, and they save what the two save histories select. The model of those sessions then runs once for each sample, with the sample's parameters, and any start that a model samples, and with every variable within the timesteps drawn again, as data is in a predictive check: an action imputed in the fit is simulated like the others. What the sessions return is kept. The sessions have at most the number of timesteps of their data, and fewer when a stopping_criterion of a model is met, since no timestep of a predictive session has data. For a prior predictive check, give a chain sampled from the prior, such as sample(model, Prior(), 1000).\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsimulated = predict_session_values(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice))\n\n#The simulated choices of participant P1, for every sample\nsimulated[@varname(P1.choice)]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.predict_session_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain","page":"API","text":"session_parameters_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                         iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                         chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\nReturn the parameters of every fitted session, for every sample, as a FlexiChain with the same samples as the fit, named after the session: P1.learning_rate for a parameter of the cognitive model, and P1.environment.mean_reward for one of the environment model. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads.\n\nresults is a matrix with what turing_full_model returns for each sample, as returned(model, chain) gives it, with the MultipleSessionsInfo of the model. The parameters are those the parameters models gave each session, so a parameters model other than IndependentSessionParameters gives parameters that aren't in the chain itself, such as one value for every session from IdenticalSessionParameters, and values from FixedSessionParameters that were never sampled. The second form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by turing_full_model and a chain sampled from it, and numbers the samples as the chain does. The result merges with the chain of session_values_chain, which has the same samples, with merge.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\n#The learning rate of participant P1, for every sample\nparameters[@varname(P1.learning_rate)]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.session_values_chain","page":"API","text":"session_values_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                     iter_indices=1:size(results, 1), chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_values_chain(results; iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                     chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_values_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                     environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nReturn what fitted sessions saved, for every sample, as a FlexiChain from FlexiChains.jl with the same samples as the fit, so that it can be summarised and plotted like the chain itself. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads. The names of the entries are the VarNames that FlexiChains.jl uses, written as @varname(P1.expected_value) with FlexiChains.jl 0.6, which Turing.jl uses, and as @vn(P1.expected_value) with 0.7.\n\nresults is a matrix with one element for each sample, iterations down and chains across, as returned(model, chain) gives it in Turing.jl. Its elements are what one of three models returns:\n\nturing_full_model, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo. The entries of each session are named after its identifier, such as P1.expected_value. The parameters of the sessions are a chain of their own, see session_parameters_chain, and what a downstream model returned isn't included.\nturing_multiple_behavioural_sessions, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo, named in the same way.\nturing_behavioural_session, given without a session info, with no session identifier in the names, such as expected_value.\n\nIn a model of one's own around these, that model must return what they return, for returned to give it.\n\nWhat each session saved, as its SaveHistorys selected, is one entry for each name, with the session's identifier first. The cognitive model's values are named after the attribute alone, since a model uses a name for one attribute only: a state as P1.expected_value, an observation as P1.reward and an action as P1.choice. An action distribution is named like its action, so it is under its kind, as P1.action_distributions.choice. The environment's values are under environment, in the same way: P1.environment.level for a state, P1.environment.reward for an observation it gave, P1.environment.choice for an action it received and P1.environment.observation_distributions.reward. A saved state that isn't a NamedTuple is one entry, P1.states or P1.environment.states. Each entry holds a vector over the timesteps for every sample, which starts with timestep 0 when the initial values are saved, so that element t + 1 is timestep t, as everywhere in the package. The observations, the actions and the distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep, so a summary of those entries needs timestep 0 left out. An identifier from several session columns is joined with underscores, as in S1_2. iter_indices and chain_indices number the samples as the chain that was fitted does.\n\nThe third form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by one of the three functions, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it. It makes a copy of the model whose sessions save what the two save histories select, gives it to returned, and makes the chain of the results, numbered as chain is. A fit is fastest when its sessions save nothing, and what they saved is then read afterwards in this way. Selecting anything in environment_save_history makes every session run its environment, see SessionInfo.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsession_values = session_values_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_value))\n\n#The expected values of participant P2 over the timesteps, for every sample\nsession_values[@varname(P2.expected_value)]\n\n#The parameters of the sessions, in the same chain\neverything = merge(session_values, session_parameters_chain(model, chain))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_values_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.apply_model","page":"API","text":"apply_model(model::AbstractModel, context::ModelContext)::ModelOutput\n\nRun a single timestep of model: update its state, and return what its outputs are sampled from. For a cognitive model the outputs are its actions, and for an environment model the observations it gives.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A simulation samples the outputs itself, from the output_distribution of the ModelOutput that the method returns, so an output that isn't random is wrapped in a Deterministic. Anything else that is random, such as a change of the state, should be sampled from context.rng, so that a simulation can be reproduced. If states are mutated in place, a prepare_state_for_saving method must be added. For use within PPLs, there may be special constraints: see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: BernoulliLogit\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n    #Inverse temperature parameter\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁)\n\n    ## Compute action probabilities ##\n    #Softmax function, instantiated as a Bernoulli logit distribution\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(β * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1, inverse_temperature = 2.0)\ncontext = ModelContext((; reward = 1.0), (; expected_reward = 0.5), (; choice = NoAction()), parameters)\n\nCognitiveModeling.apply_model(RescorlaWagner(), context).new_state\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.55,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.apply_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_state","page":"API","text":"initialize_state(model::AbstractModel, parameters)\n\nCreate the initial state that model has before its first timestep, which can depend on parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without a state returns an empty NamedTuple, (;).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n           return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(RescorlaWagner(), (; initial_value = 0.5))\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.attribute_names","page":"API","text":"attribute_names(model::AbstractModel)::ModelAttributes\n\nReturn the names of the parameters, states, inputs and outputs of model, in a ModelAttributes.\n\nThe names define the model, so they must match what the model uses. The parameters are the names that apply_model and initialize_state read from the parameters. The states are the names in the state that initialize_state returns and in new_state, which apply_model reads from context.previous_state. The inputs are the names that apply_model reads from context.input, and the outputs are the names in output_distribution, which are also those in context.previous_output. For a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. Nothing checks that they match while the model runs.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without one kind of attribute gives () for it, and so does one whose state isn't a NamedTuple. simulate and InstantiatedModel use the output names for the output before the first timestep, and check the parameters, the previous output and a NamedTuple state against the names. A simulation with an environment also checks that the two models fit together: the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other, unless a NameMap says which of them are passed on, and under which names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(RescorlaWagner()).parameters\n(:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.attribute_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving","page":"API","text":"prepare_state_for_saving(model::AbstractModel, state)\n\nReturn what a simulation saves of state, the state of a cognitive model or of an environment model, as it is about to save it. The state that the next timestep starts from isn't affected.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns state as it is, so nothing is copied. One which changes an object in its state in place must add a method which copies it, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsInPlace <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::LearnsInPlace, state)\n           return (; values = copy(state.values))\n       end;\n\njulia> state = (; values = [0.5, 0.5]);\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(LearnsInPlace(), state).values === state.values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.default_parameters","page":"API","text":"default_parameters(model::AbstractModel)\n\nReturn the default parameter values of a cognitive model or of an environment model, such as (; learning_rate = 0.1), which InstantiatedModel and simulate use for the parameters they aren't given.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so there are no default values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.default_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion","page":"API","text":"stopping_criterion(model::AbstractModel, state, parameters)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, has ended, with state as its state after a timestep and its parameters, so that a simulation stops there instead of running all of n_steps.\n\nsimulate and simulate! check it for both models before each timestep, with the states that the timestep before left, unless they are given check_stopping_criterion = false. When it returns true for either, no further timestep runs, and what is returned and added to the histories has the timesteps that ran. When the environment starts, the cognitive model has then made its last observation and acted on it, and the environment doesn't receive that action. A model whose criterion is already met before the first timestep can't be simulated, and throws an error: a new simulation starts from the initial state, and reset! puts an InstantiatedModel back there. A fitted session checks it as SessionInfo describes.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns false, so a simulation runs all of its timesteps, and the check then costs nothing.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Maze <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(environment::Maze, state, parameters)\n           return state.position == parameters.goal\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(Maze(), (; position = 4), (; goal = 4))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model","page":"API","text":"turing_apply_model(model::AbstractModel, context::ModelContext)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model for a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, to be used as a submodel, which returns a ModelOutput. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls apply_model. A model which samples within a timestep needs to add a method, however; or if any other changes are desired within Turing. It must sample with ~ instead of from context.rng. Otherwise the model has a different log density each time it is evaluated, with no error. The same holds for an environment model, which fitting uses to fill in observations that are MissingValue in the data: what it samples itself, such as a change of its state, must be sampled with ~, while fitting samples the observations from what it returns.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct NoisyRescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model(\n        model::NoisyRescorlaWagner,\n        context::ModelContext,\n    )\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Sample the noise in the update\n    ϵ ~ Normal(0, context.parameters.noise)\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁) + ϵ\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state","page":"API","text":"turing_initialize_state(model::AbstractModel, parameters)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which gives the state of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, before the first timestep, to be used as a submodel, as initialize_state does. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls initialize_state, which is enough for most models, since a starting state that is fitted is simply one that is copied from a parameter there: a fitted session runs it with the parameters of each evaluation, so a prior on the parameter is a prior on the starting state. A model which samples something of its own when it initializes needs to add a method, and sample it with ~, as for turing_apply_model. Its variables are named under initial_cognitive_state, or initial_environment_state for an environment model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct UncertainStart <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state(\n        model::UncertainStart,\n        parameters,\n    )\n\n    #Sample where the expected reward starts, around the parameter for it\n    V₀ ~ Normal(parameters.initial_value, parameters.initial_noise)\n\n    return (; expected_reward = V₀)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_session_parameters_model","page":"API","text":"turing_session_parameters_model(parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model which gives the parameters of every session in sessions_info, from a description of how they are given or sampled, for the cognitive model or the environment model of a turing_full_model. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model must return a NamedTuple with one entry for each parameter, named as the model names it, and a vector in each with one value for every session, in the order of the sessions. Each subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel that comes with the package has a method.\n\nImplementation\n\nA parameters model of another kind, such as a hierarchical model or a regression, is a subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel with a method of this function, which can read n_sessions and the identifiers of the sessions from sessions_info, and anything else from its own fields, such as the covariates of a regression. The full model treats it as one that samples, so that with it the environment of every session runs, and the observations in the data inform the environment's parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_session_parameters_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model","page":"API","text":"turing_downstream_model(downstream_model, saved_behavioural_values, cognitive_parameters,\n                        sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model which runs after the sessions in a turing_full_model, from a description of it, with what the sessions saved, the cognitive parameters of every session as the parameters model returned them, and the sessions' sessions_info. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model can return anything, which the full model returns as its downstream_values. NoDownstreamModel is the default, with a method which samples nothing and returns nothing.\n\nImplementation\n\nA downstream model is a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel with a method of this function, which can ignore any of the arguments. A regression of some measurement on a state of the cognitive model, for instance, reads the state from saved_behavioural_values, a vector with the saved values of each session as turing_behavioural_session returns them, so the cognitive_save_history of the sessions must select that state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.n_sessions","page":"API","text":"n_sessions(sessions_info)\n\nReturn how many sessions a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which a parameters model gives the parameters of. Used by turing_session_parameters_model methods, such as the one for IndependentSessionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.n_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.session_index","page":"API","text":"session_index(sessions_info, session_id)\n\nReturn the index i of the session with the identifier session_id in a MultipleSessionsInfo, so that its variables are under session[i], its saved values are element i of what turing_multiple_behavioural_sessions returns, and its parameters are element i of each vector a parameters model gives. The identifier is the value in the session column, or a NamedTuple of the values when there are several, as in sessions_info.session_ids. Used by users reading a chain, not by the models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"text/plain\")}, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"API","text":"show(io::IO, ::MIME\"text/plain\", model::AbstractModel)\n\nPrint a summary of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, and the names of its attributes.\n\nThis is what the REPL and notebooks show. Everywhere else, such as in print or inside a vector, a model is printed on one line by Julia's default show(io, model).\n\nThe attributes show as not specified if model has no method of attribute_names, and a kind of attribute shows as none if the model has no names of that kind.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleModel <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::ExampleModel)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :noise),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = (),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleModel()\n-- Cognitive model --\nType: ExampleModel\nParameters: learning_rate, noise\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> struct ExampleEnvironment <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::ExampleEnvironment)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = :reward_probability,\n               states = (),\n               inputs = :choice,\n               outputs = :reward,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleEnvironment()\n-- Environment model --\nType: ExampleEnvironment\nParameters: reward_probability\nStates: none\nInputs: choice\nOutputs: reward\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"text/plain\")}, SessionInfo}","page":"API","text":"show(io::IO, ::MIME\"text/plain\", session_info::SessionInfo)\nshow(io::IO, ::MIME\"text/plain\", sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nPrint a summary of a session, or of several, on one line each: the models, the timesteps or the sessions, the names in the data, and who starts. This is what the REPL and notebooks show, since the full type and the data would fill the screen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/compiling_with_reactant/#Compiling-simulations-with-Reactant.jl","page":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to compile a simulation with Reactant.jl, and how to take its gradient with Enzyme.jl. Until then, this is what to know before trying.\n\nShort simulations can be compiled with Reactant.jl, if the action distributions work with traced numbers, which those from Distributions.jl don't. Compile a function that takes a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument and passes it on as rng, because @jit doesn't trace keyword arguments, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run. Compile times grow quickly with n_steps, so this is for a few hundred timesteps at most.\n\nThe page Automatic differentiation and Reactant lists what is tested, and the pitfalls a model can run into.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_actr_models/#Models-from-ACTRModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use ACTRModels.jl, a package for building ACT-R models, inside a cognitive model.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Placeholder tutorial. Each file in docs/literate/ is a plain Julia script that becomes a page like this one when the documentation is built.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_sequential_sampling_models/#Models-from-SequentialSamplingModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use the sequential sampling models of SequentialSamplingModels.jl, such as the drift diffusion model, as what a cognitive model samples its actions from, so that it gives both a choice and a response time.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Models-from-POMDPs.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"POMDPs.jl describes a task as a problem object, and has solvers which give a policy for it, and updaters which keep a belief about the hidden state. A cognitive model can keep such a belief as its state, update it with their updater, and choose from the values their policy gives each action.\n\nThis page shows such a cognitive model. It doesn't simulate or fit it yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, to keep the documentation's environment small. It was run by hand with POMDPs.jl 1.0, POMDPTools.jl 1.1 and QMDP.jl 0.1.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: DiscreteNonParametric\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing POMDPs: POMDPs\nusing POMDPTools: DiscreteUpdater, actionvalues\nusing QMDP: QMDPSolver","title":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#A-Bayesian-observer-with-a-solved-policy","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"The cognitive model holds the problem, a policy for it and a belief updater. The problem is solved once, when the cognitive model is created, so nothing is solved on a timestep.\n\nstruct BayesianObserver{Tpomdp,Tpomdp_policy,Tpomdp_updater} <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    pomdp::Tpomdp\n    pomdp_policy::Tpomdp_policy\n    pomdp_updater::Tpomdp_updater\nend\n\nfunction BayesianObserver(pomdp)\n    return BayesianObserver(\n        pomdp, POMDPs.solve(QMDPSolver(), pomdp), DiscreteUpdater(pomdp)\n    )\nend\n\nIts state is the belief, which starts as the problem's distribution over initial states.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::BayesianObserver, parameters)\n    return (;\n        belief=POMDPs.initialize_belief(\n            model.pomdp_updater, POMDPs.initialstate(model.pomdp)\n        )\n    )\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the belief is updated from the previous action and the observation that followed it, and the action probabilities are a softmax over the values the policy gives each action under that belief, with the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl. On the first timestep there is no previous action, which AbstractNoValue covers, and the belief is left as it is.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::BayesianObserver, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    belief = input.previous_state.belief\n    previous_choice = input.previous_action.choice\n    β = input.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Update the belief from the previous action and what was observed after it\n    if !(previous_choice isa AbstractNoValue)\n        belief = POMDPs.update(\n            model.pomdp_updater, belief, previous_choice, input.observation.observation\n        )\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The value the policy gives each action under the belief\n    Qₜ = actionvalues(model.pomdp_policy, belief)\n    #Softmax over the action values, as a distribution over the problem's own actions\n    Pₜ = DiscreteNonParametric(collect(POMDPs.actions(model.pomdp)), softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice=Pₜ), (; belief))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::BayesianObserver)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=:inverse_temperature,\n        states=:belief,\n        observations=:observation,\n        actions=:choice,\n    )\nend\n\nThe updater returns a new belief on each timestep, so the saved states need no copying.\n\nA problem from POMDPModels.jl gives a cognitive model for its task, such as BayesianObserver(TigerPOMDP()). The problem can be used as the environment too, through CommonRLEnvironment. That environment also gives reward and terminated, which this cognitive model doesn't list, so a NameMap must select the observation for it: observation_map = NameMap(:observation).\n\nThe learners in TabularTDLearning.jl can't be used this way. They are solvers which run every episode themselves, so there is no single timestep to call.","title":"A Bayesian observer with a solved policy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Fitting","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive model yet. When it is fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for this cognitive model, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. So did ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl, while Mooncake.jl and Enzyme's forward mode threw an error.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax got a wrong gradient for this cognitive model from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"#CognitiveModeling.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for computational modeling of cognition and behaviour. It is under development.","title":"CognitiveModeling.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_common_rl/#Interfacing-with-CommonRLInterface.jl","page":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl as the environment of a cognitive model, which also covers the environments of POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl, since they convert to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl"}]
}
